export const lpContent={
'happy-sad-angry-calm':{
 title:'The Happy, Sad, Angry & Calm Lesson',one:'The four feelings a day is made of — how to name them, make their faces and find your way back to calm.',
 seoTitle:'Feelings Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Naming Happy, Sad, Angry and Calm (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four first feelings — happy, sad, angry and calm — with a face game, a body scan and a calm-down breath, the first lesson of social-emotional learning.',
 intro:'This lesson opens the whole class the way every feelings teacher does: with the four faces your child already makes every day, and the words that go with them.',
 what:'Happy, sad, angry and calm are the four first feelings — the ones every child meets before they can tie a shoe. Each has its own face, its own body and its own speed: happy spreads, sad droops, angry flashes hot, calm slows everything down.',
 mean:'For a small child, feelings arrive as weather — big, real and unexplained. Naming them is the first act of social-emotional learning: a feeling with a name becomes something to talk about instead of something that just happens to you.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: every feeling has a FACE (smiles, frowns, eyebrows, soft eyes); every feeling has a BODY (bouncy, droopy, hot, still); and every feeling is allowed — even angry — as long as hands stay kind.',
 why:'These four words are the load-bearing walls of the whole class: every later card — the tricky ones, the calm-down skills, the kindness words — stands on the ability to say happy, sad, angry or calm and mean it.',
 dev:'Emotion vocabulary (the foundation of self-regulation), face-reading (the first empathy skill), body awareness, and the naming habit that turns feelings into words instead of meltdowns.',
 vocab:'happy, sad, angry, calm, feeling, face, calm down',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four feelings live on this card — let us meet them."\n2) Name each face slowly: happy smiles, sad droops, angry shows its eyebrows, calm closes its eyes. Let your child copy each face in a small mirror or at your face.\n3) Play feelings charades: you make one of the four faces, your child names it and makes it back. Swap roles.\n4) Do the body check: "Where does happy live in your body? Where does angry live?" No wrong answers — chests and tummies are common.\n5) Finish with the calm breath below and the takeaway: "Every feeling is okay — and calm is the one we can visit on purpose."',
 say:'"Everybody has feelings — happy days, sad days, angry moments and calm ones, and grown-ups have all four too. Happy is a smile that wants to spread. Sad is when something is gone or wrong. Angry is the hot one — it shows in the eyebrows! And calm is the slow, soft one we can find on purpose with a big breath. All four are allowed. All four have names. And naming them is your superpower."',
 notice:'On this card four panels each show one labeled feeling face: Happy, a boy with a big open smile; Sad, a boy with a downturned mouth; Angry, a boy with furrowed brows; and Calm, a boy with closed eyes and a soft smile — the four faces exactly as they arrive on a small person.',
 ask:'"Which face is the smiley one?" "What does angry look like — show me the eyebrows!" "Which feeling closes its eyes?" "Which one do you feel right now?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Angry has angry eyebrows!" "Calm is the sleeping one" or the proud one: "I am happy RIGHT NOW."',
 real:'Feelings live everywhere once you hunt them: characters in books (find the angry face!), people at the bus stop, the dog\u2019s wagging tail-happy and the cat\u2019s annoyed-flat ears. One "look, that man looks calm" on a walk is worth a worksheet.',
 play:'Four-Corners Feelings: label the room\u2019s corners happy, sad, angry, calm. You call a tiny story — "we got ice cream!" "the tower fell!" — and your child runs to the matching corner, making the face on arrival. Five stories, four corners, one giggling vocabulary lesson.',
 tips:'If angry becomes the only interesting face, lean into calm instead — make it the mysterious, cozy one: "calm is the feeling cats know best." And never rush the body check: children who know WHERE a feeling lives can spot it arriving earlier next time.',
 check:'Ask: "Which feeling has angry eyebrows? Which one closes its eyes? Are all four feelings allowed?" Angry, calm, yes — three answers and the first lesson of the class is won.'},
'kind-share-help-thank-you':{
 title:'The Kind, Share, Help & Thank You Lesson',one:'The four first kind words — and how a heart, a book, a helping hand and a thank you grow a kinder day.',
 seoTitle:'Kindness Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Kind, Share, Help and Thank You (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four first kindness words — kind, share, help and thank you — with a catching game, a helping-mission and a gratitude habit that sticks.',
 intro:'This lesson turns four abstract words into four things your child can DO before lunch — because at four, kindness is a verb, not a poster.',
 what:'Kind, share, help and thank you are the first four kindness words: kind is the friendly heart everything else grows from, share lets someone use what is yours, help lends a hand to someone who needs it and thank you says "I saw what you did."',
 mean:'Kindness words teach children that their actions have weather — they change how other people feel. That discovery ("I made her happy!") is the seed every later social skill grows from.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: kind is a HEART first (you notice people); share and help are HANDS (they do things); thank you is a MOUTH that closes the circle by naming the kindness out loud.',
 why:'These four arrive second on the card path because they are the words a child can use within the hour — breakfast offers a share, socks offer a helper, and every gift offers a thank you. Early wins build the kindness habit.',
 dev:'Pro-social behaviour (the psychology name for kindness in action), generosity, perspective-taking beginnings, gratitude practice and the warmth of being caught doing good.',
 vocab:'kind, share, help, thank you, kind words, give, friendly',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four kind words — each one a thing you can DO."\n2) Walk the panels: the boy with folded hands is feeling kind, the girl hands a book to SHARE, the big kid HELPS the small one up, the girl says THANK YOU with clasped hands.\n3) Play kindness catch: toss a soft ball, and whoever catches it names one kind thing they saw today — any size counts.\n4) Assign one helper mission: "today your job is to help ONE person or toy." Report back at dinner.\n5) Close with the thank-you circle below and the takeaway: "kind hearts notice, kind hands do, kind mouths say thank you."',
 say:'"Kind is the warmest word there is. It means your heart is friendly and your eyes are open — you NOTICE when someone needs something. Share means letting someone in on what is yours. Help means lending your hands to someone whose hands are full. And thank you is the word that tells them: I saw your kindness, and it made my day better. You have all four in you already — let us go use them."',
 notice:'On this card four panels show the words in action: Kind, a boy with both hands folded on his chest; Share, a girl handing a book to a friend; Help, a child steadying a smaller boy; and Thank You, a girl with hands clasped — each kindness shown by a child, not explained.',
 ask:'"What is the boy with folded hands feeling?" "What is the girl doing with the book?" "Who is helping whom?" "When did YOU say thank you last?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Sharing is letting my friend use it!" "I helped the baby!" or the observation: "The small boy fell and the big one helped him up."',
 real:'Kindness opportunities stack up all day: crackers to split, a sibling\u2019s dropped cup, the neighbour\u2019s bins, the librarian\u2019s returned book. Narrate the ones you catch — "that was KIND" — and the words attach themselves to real moments.',
 play:'The Kindness Museum: at dinner, everyone places an invisible exhibit — "this is the time I shared my crayons" — and the family claps. exhibits can be from ANYONE\u2019S day, including sightings of other people\u2019s kindness. The museum never closes; it reopens every dinner.',
 tips:'Never force the sharing of a truly precious thing — children learn generosity from what they CHOOSE, and guarding one special toy is allowed even in kind families. And catch yourself being kind out loud: "I am going to help Dad carry these" is a lesson in a sentence.',
 check:'Ask: "Which word means lending a hand? Which word do we say when someone gives? Is kind a heart-word or a hand-word?" Help, thank you, both — three answers and the kind words are theirs.'},
'excited-worried-surprised-proud':{
 title:'The Excited, Worried, Surprised & Proud Lesson',one:'Four bigger feelings with four different bodies — the wiggles, the tight tummy, the popped OH and the tall smile.',
 seoTitle:'Feelings Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Excited, Worried, Surprised and Proud (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach four bigger feeling words — excited, worried, surprised and proud — with a body-acting game and a feelings hunt, growing emotion vocabulary.',
 intro:'This lesson grows the vocabulary past the first four: feelings with bigger bodies and longer names, met in a book, a birthday and a wobbly first day.',
 what:'Excited, worried, surprised and proud are four bigger feelings: excited is joy with the volume up, worried is a what-if that will not hush, surprised is the feeling of OH, and proud is the tall feeling of a thing done well.',
 mean:'Bigger feelings bring bigger bodies — wiggles, tight tummies, jumped chins, straightened backs. Children who can read those body signals early learn to know what they feel BEFORE it becomes behaviour.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: feelings show up in the BODY before the MOUTH (wiggles mean excited, tight means worried); surprised is the shortest feeling and proud is the longest; and worried is normal — everyone\u2019s tummy talks sometimes.',
 why:'These four bridge the everyday words and the tricky ones later in the class — worried especially, which is the feeling behind a thousand bedtime stalling tactics and one brave first day of school.',
 dev:'Interoception (sensing the body\u2019s signals), emotion differentiation (telling feelings apart), self-awareness, and story comprehension — feelings words double as reading-comprehension tools the moment characters have problems.',
 vocab:'excited, worried, surprised, proud, feelings, tummy, brave',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four bigger feelings — feel them in your BODY."\n2) Act each panel: the huge grin (excited), the anxious eyebrows (worried), the round O mouth (surprised), the crossed arms and tall smile (proud).\n3) Play the body game: call a feeling, everyone shows it with their WHOLE body, not just the face.\n4) Tell one tiny story per feeling: the night before a birthday (excited), the first day of school (worried), a jack-in-the-box (surprised), a finished tower (proud).\n5) Close with the hunt below and the takeaway: "your body talks about feelings — now you can speak body."',
 say:'"Some feelings are big and some are sneaky. Excited is the wiggle one — your legs simply cannot keep still. Worried is the one that lives in the tummy and whispers what-if. Surprised is the fastest feeling in the world — it lasts one second and your mouth goes OH. And proud is the strongest one: it straightens your back when you have done something hard. Feel them all. Name them all. They are all yours."',
 notice:'On this card four panels show the four faces: Excited, a boy with a huge open-mouthed grin; Worried, a girl with drawn-up eyebrows; Surprised, a boy with a round open mouth; and Proud, a girl with crossed arms and a tall smile — four feelings, four very different faces.',
 ask:'"Which feeling makes you wiggle?" "Where does worried live — the tummy or the toes?" "Which feeling says OH?" "What made you feel proud this week?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Excited is too much happy!" "My tummy does worried" or the story: "I was proud when I zipped my own coat."',
 real:'Feelings previews are everywhere: the wiggles before a party, the quiet before a haircut, the OH at a magic trick, the tall walk after a new skill. Name them in the moment — "those are excited wiggles!" — and the vocabulary sticks to real weather.',
 play:'Feelings Freeze: music plays, everyone dances; when it stops you call a feeling and everyone freezes AS that feeling — a proud freeze, a worried freeze, a surprised freeze. Last one standing is the Feelings Professor and calls the next round.',
 tips:'Worried deserves extra gentleness — never laugh a worry away; write it down, say it back, and answer one what-if with one real plan. And when your child acts proud, resist the urge to temper it: tall smiles at four are exactly the right size.',
 check:'Ask: "Which feeling is the fast OH one? Which one lives in the tummy? Which one straightens your back?" Surprised, worried, proud — three answers and the bigger words are landing.'},
'listen-gentle-take-turns-kind-words':{
 title:'The Listen, Gentle, Take Turns & Kind Words Lesson',one:'The four habits that make play gentle — ears, hands, turns and words that friends feel good about.',
 seoTitle:'Social Skills Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Listen, Gentle, Turns and Kind Words (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach four gentle habits — listening, gentle hands, taking turns and kind words — with a turn-taking game and a kind-words jar, the daily skills of friendship.',
 intro:'This lesson is the manners course behind every good playdate: four habits that make a child the one others want to play with again tomorrow.',
 what:'Listen, gentle, take turns and kind words are the four daily habits of friendship: listening ears, gentle hands, turn-taking patience and words that leave people feeling bigger, not smaller.',
 mean:'Play is a child\u2019s first workplace, and these four habits are its professional skills — children who practise them get invited back, and every invitation is another chance to practise.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: listening means your MOUTH waits while your EARS work; gentle is a hands-and-words setting (soft with people, pets and small things); and turns always come back — waiting is just the queue, not the end.',
 why:'These four are the most-used words in any preschool classroom — teachers say them fifty times a day because they are the actual mechanics of group play. Learning them at home means the classroom feels already-familiar.',
 dev:'Self-control (waiting), impulse management (gentle hands), turn-taking (the backbone of games and conversation), and prosocial language — the difference between "gimme" and "may I".',
 vocab:'listen, gentle, take turns, kind words, wait, soft, my turn',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four habits that make you a great friend to play with."\n2) Read the panels: the boy with his hand cupped to his ear is LISTENING; the girl with wrapped arms is BE GENTLE; the raised finger and waiting hands are TAKE TURNS; the smiling girl is on KIND WORDS.\n3) Drill each one in a minute: one whole song of listening, one gentle stroke of a soft toy, three turns of a rolling ball, one kind word each at the table.\n4) Play the turn game below with the words spoken out loud — "my turn… your turn."\n5) Close with the takeaway: "gentle gets remembered; kind gets invited back."',
 say:'"Listen means your ears are the boss and your mouth is the helper — ears first, mouth second. Gentle means soft: soft hands on people, soft hands on pets, soft hands on the things people love. Take turns means the waiting is just part of the game — your turn always rolls back around, like the ball does. And kind words are the words that make people glow: well done, good try, want to play? Four habits. Practice them a minute a day and you will be the friend everyone asks for."',
 notice:'On this card four panels show the habits: Listen, a boy cupping a hand to his ear; Be Gentle, a girl wrapping her arms around herself; Take turns, a boy with a finger raised while a girl waits; and Use kind words, a girl speaking warmly — each habit shown at child size.',
 ask:'"What are the boy\u2019s ears doing?" "How do gentle hands touch?" "What comes after MY turn?" "What kind word could you say right now?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Gentle is soft hands!" "Then it\u2019s YOUR turn" or the generous one: "you can go first."',
 real:'The habits live in real minutes: listening to the whole instructions before building, patting the cat gently, waiting for the swing, saying "good job!" when a sibling finally manages the zip. Each one takes ten seconds and they stack into a reputation.',
 play:'Pass-the-Talk: a spoon gets passed around the table, and only the holder speaks — everyone else practises listening ears. The holder says one kind word about the person on their left, then passes. Two rounds and the kind words start generating themselves.',
 tips:'Gentle hands work better as a reminder BEFORE the wobble than a correction after — whisper "gentle hands" as the pet approaches. And let your child catch YOU taking turns: "you waited for me — thank you!" reverses the teaching and doubles it.',
 check:'Ask: "Which habit is ears-first? What do gentle hands do? Does your turn come back?" Listening, soft, yes — three answers and the habits are in play.'},
'breathe-brave-try-again-i-can-do-it':{
 title:'The Breathe, Brave, Try Again & I Can Do It Lesson',one:'The four-step courage ladder — one breath, one brave thought, one more try, one small voice saying I can.',
 seoTitle:'Courage Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Breathe, Be Brave, Try Again, I Can Do It (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four-step courage ladder — breathe, be brave, try again, I can do it — turning wobbly moments into climbable ones, building resilience.',
 intro:'This lesson turns courage from a personality into a ladder: four rungs any child can climb the next time something feels too big.',
 what:'Breathe, brave, try again and I can do it are the four rungs of the courage ladder: a breath to steady the body, a brave thought to steady the mind, one more try to steady the habit and the little sentence that holds it all up.',
 mean:'Big feelings shrink when the body calms first — that is why breathe comes BEFORE brave. Children who learn the order stop waiting to feel brave and discover that brave is something you DO, one breath at a time.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: the breath comes FIRST (in like a flower, out like a candle); brave is a thought you THINK, not a feeling you wait for; and try again is the rung you may climb as many times as you like.',
 why:'This ladder is the first calm-down tool of the class, and it is reused by every later card — the pause, the count to ten, the reset. Learning it early means the harder moments later have somewhere to stand.',
 dev:'Self-regulation (the breath), courage as process, growth mindset (try again is the plantable phrase), and self-talk — the sentence I can do it is the first one children learn to say to themselves.',
 vocab:'breathe, brave, try again, I can do it, brave breath, steady, courage',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "This is the courage ladder — four steps up a wobbly moment."\n2) Climb it slowly: hands at the chest for BREATHE (in like a flower, out like a candle), fists up for BE BRAVE, forward lean for TRY AGAIN, fist up small and certain for I CAN DO IT.\n3) Pick one real wobbly thing — the big slide, a new food, the dark hallway — and climb the ladder out loud together.\n4) Tell the story of something YOU found scary and climbed: grown-up honesty is the best poster.\n5) Close with the game below and the takeaway: "brave is not a feeling you wait for — it is four steps you take."',
 say:'"When something feels too big, we do not have to be the bravest person in the world — we just have to climb the ladder. Step one: breathe. In like you are smelling a flower, out like you are blowing a candle — do it twice. Step two: think brave — what would the brave version try? Step three: try again — one more go, wobbles allowed. Step four, the best one: say it, small and strong — I can do it. Because you can. And if the first try wobbles? The ladder is still there. It never runs out."',
 notice:'On this card four panels climb the ladder: Breathe, a boy with hands folded at his chest mid-breath; Be Brave, a girl in a little cape with her fist raised; Try Again, a determined boy with a raised fist; and I Can Do It, a confident girl with a small fist up — the four steps exactly as your child will climb them.',
 ask:'"What comes first — the breath or the brave?" "What does brave look like on this card?" "How many times may we try again?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Flower breath, candle breath!" "I can do it!" (the whole point) or the honest one: "I was scared and I did it anyway."',
 real:'Wobbly moments queue up daily: the tall slide, the swimming step, the new food touching the old food, the goodbye at preschool\u2019s door. Climb the ladder at each one — quietly, quickly — and by the tenth climb your child starts it without you.',
 play:'Ladder Tag for Toys: line up three stuffed animals at the "too big slide" (a chair with a cushion). Each animal climbs the ladder out loud — breathe, brave, try again, I can do it — and takes the slide. Your child voices all three: shy voice, shaky voice, then the triumphant one. The third animal always makes it look easy.',
 tips:'Say the ladder with your child, never AT them — "let us climb it together" beats "just be brave" every single time. And celebrate the climb, not the landing: a child who breathed, thought brave and tried again SUCCEEDED, whatever the slide did.',
 check:'Ask: "What is step one of the ladder? What do we say at the top? Does the ladder run out?" Breathe, I can do it, never — three answers and the ladder is theirs to climb.'},
'smile-care-forgive-friend':{
 title:'The Smile, Care, Forgive & Friend Lesson',one:'The friendship ladder — a smile to start it, care to warm it, forgive to mend it and friend at the top.',
 seoTitle:'Friendship Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Smile, Care, Forgive and Friend (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the friendship ladder — smile, care, forgive, friend — with a mending game that shows how friendships repair, the foundation of healthy friendships.',
 intro:'This lesson walks the friendship ladder in order — from the first smile that opens a door to the forgiving word that keeps a friendship mended and running.',
 what:'Smile, care, forgive and friend are the friendship words: a smile starts things, care keeps people warm, forgive mends the cracks after fights and friend is the name for everything the first three built.',
 mean:'Fights are not friendship failures — they are friendship weather. The child who knows the mending word (forgive) learns that friendships survive storms, which makes them braver friends and calmer fighters.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: a smile is the friendliest thing you can CARRY; care means noticing when someone is sad or hurt; and forgive is the magic restart word — it turns a broken moment back into a game.',
 why:'Every playground friendship runs this ladder weekly. Children who only know the top (friend) but not the mending rung lose friendships to ordinary weather — this lesson hands them the repair tool.',
 dev:'Relationship repair (the skill under every lasting friendship), empathy beginnings (care), conflict recovery, and the social courage of the first smile.',
 vocab:'smile, care, forgive, friend, sorry, mend, start again',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "This is how friendships are built — and repaired."\n2) Climb the ladder: the beaming boy SMILES, the girl hugging a heart CARES, the peaceful boy FORGIVES with calm hands, and the waving girl is the FRIEND at the top.\n3) Tell a friendship weather story: two friends, one squabble, one sorry, one "I forgive you" — and the game continues.\n4) Play the mending game below with puppets so the repair script gets rehearsed before it is needed.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "every good friend has forgiven something — that is what the word really means."',
 say:'"Here is the secret about friends: they still fight sometimes. That is normal — friends are just people who know how to find each other again afterwards. A smile starts it: hello, I am friendly. Care keeps it warm: I notice when you are sad, and I sit with you. Forgive mends it: after a fight, someone says sorry and someone says I forgive you — and the game begins again. That is the whole ladder. Smile, care, forgive — and the top rung, friend, takes care of itself."',
 notice:'On this card four panels climb the ladder: Smile, a boy beaming; Care, a girl hugging a big pink heart; Forgive, a boy with peaceful closed eyes and clasped hands; and Friend, a girl waving hello — the four rungs drawn at child height.',
 ask:'"What does a smile say without words?" "What do we do when a friend is sad?" "What is the word that starts a game again after a fight?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Forgive means start again!" "I care about my teddy" or the mended one: "we fought but we are still friends."',
 real:'Friendship weather rolls in daily: the squabble over the yellow crayon, the friend who did not come to the party, the bumped tower. Say the ladder out loud in the small storms — "should we smile, care or forgive here?" — and the big storms come with the words already installed.',
 play:'Puppet Mend-It: two puppets want the same car. Fight! Then your child runs the repair: one puppet apologises, one says "I forgive you," and they invent a two-puppet game with the car. Swap roles and run it again — by round three the script is theirs and the fighting is just weather.',
 tips:'Do not rush the forgive — it only works when the sorry is real. If your child is not ready, the pause is allowed: "we will find the forgiving word when we are ready." And model it yourself: children who hear grown-ups say "I am sorry I snapped — do you forgive me?" learn that mending runs uphill and downhill both.',
 check:'Ask: "Which rung mends a fight? Which rung starts a friendship? Can friends fight and stay friends?" Forgive, smile, yes — three answers and the ladder is standing.'},
'lonely-loved-frustrated-relaxed':{
 title:'The Lonely, Loved, Frustrated & Relaxed Lesson',one:'Four deeper feelings — the ache, the warmth, the hot push and the slow settle — named so they never have to shout.',
 seoTitle:'Feelings Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Lonely, Loved, Frustrated, Relaxed (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach four deeper feelings — lonely, loved, frustrated and relaxed — with a bedtime sorting game, giving children the words that prevent meltdowns.',
 intro:'This lesson goes deeper than the first faces: four feelings every preschooler has carried without a name, and the relief of finally hanging words on them.',
 what:'Lonely is the ache of missing someone, loved is the warmth of being held, frustrated is the hot push when things will not cooperate and relaxed is the slow settle when everything finally fits.',
 mean:'Lonely and frustrated are the two feelings that most often come out sideways — as clinginess, as explosions over small things. Naming them gives the feeling a door: it can walk out as words instead of breaking out as behaviour.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: lonely means I want someone who is not here (and it passes when they return); frustrated means my hands and the thing are arguing (not my fault, not permanent); and relaxed is a feeling you can BUILD with slow breath and soft shoulders.',
 why:'This card is the class\u2019s honesty moment: not every feeling is sunny, and the ones that are hardest to say are the ones most worth naming. Children who can say "I am frustrated" need far fewer storms to say the same thing.',
 dev:'Emotion differentiation at depth, interoception (noticing internal states), self-advocacy (lonely can ASK for company), and down-regulation — the relaxed word introduces the skill of deliberately calming.',
 vocab:'lonely, loved, frustrated, relaxed, deep feelings, settle, calm body',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four deep feelings — some warm, some wobbly, all allowed."\n2) Read the panels: the drooping boy is LONELY, the girl hugging a heart is LOVED, the clenched-fist boy is FRUSTRATED, the serene girl is RELAXED.\n3) Give each a body shape: lonely curls small, loved wraps arms around itself, frustrated squeezes fists, relaxed melts shoulders.\n4) Play the bedtime sort below — the day\u2019s moments sorted into the four feelings, no fixing allowed.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "a feeling with a name is a feeling with a door."',
 say:'"Some feelings sit deep. Lonely is the one that whispers when you want somebody and they are not there — at preschool pickup it always ends, because here I am. Loved is the opposite: the warm hug feeling that some children carry all day in their pocket. Frustrated is the hot one — when the block falls, the zip sticks, the lid will not turn, and your fists want to shout. And relaxed is the slow one: soft shoulders, slow breath, everything okay. Lonely, loved, frustrated, relaxed — four names. Now none of them has to shout."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the deep feelings: Lonely, a drooping sad boy; Loved, a girl hugging a pink heart to her chest; Frustrated, a boy with clenched fists; and Relaxed, a girl with closed eyes and a serene face — every one drawn gently, none of them scary.',
 ask:'"Which feeling is the warm one?" "What does frustrated do with its fists?" "When did you feel relaxed today?" "What helps lonely feel better?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Frustrated is when it will not WORK!" "I feel loved right now" or the deep one: "I was lonely at naptime."',
 real:'Deep feelings visit daily: the first minutes at drop-off (lonely), the bath before bed (relaxed), the shoe that will not go on (frustrated), the bedtime cuddle (loved). Name them where they live and the words become part of the day\u2019s furniture.',
 play:'Bedtime Sort: four invisible boxes on the blanket — lonely, loved, frustrated, relaxed. Walk through the day out loud and drop each moment in its box: "the morning hug? LOVED box. The broken crayon? FRUSTRATED box." Then let your child sort YOUR day. No fixing, no lessons — the sorting is the whole game.',
 tips:'When the real frustrated arrives, lend the word, not the lecture: "this feels frustrating — the lid will not turn." The word in a warm voice works better mid-storm than any fix. And when your child says lonely, treat it as information, not criticism — company is the answer, right now, even just two minutes.',
 check:'Ask: "Which feeling is the hot fists one? Which one melts the shoulders? What is the warm hug feeling?" Frustrated, relaxed, loved — three answers and the deep words are awake.'},
'patient-honest-polite-fair':{
 title:'The Patient, Honest, Polite & Fair Lesson',one:'The four character words — the waiting body, the true mouth, the nice words and the same-for-everyone rule.',
 seoTitle:'Character Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Patient, Honest, Polite and Fair (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach four character words — patient, honest, polite and fair — with a waiting game and a fair-shares deal, the everyday character education of preschool.',
 intro:'This lesson teaches the four words grown-ups mean when they say good character — each one shrunk to something a four-year-old can practise before bedtime.',
 what:'Patient, honest, polite and fair are the character words: patient is the body that can wait, honest is the mouth that tells it true, polite is please-thank-you-excuse-me, and fair is the rule that everyone gets a turn — including the smallest.',
 mean:'Character words are identity seeds: a child who hears "you WERE patient" starts collecting evidence of being a patient person. Four words on this card, four small identities under construction.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: patient is a BODY skill (hands quiet, feet still, breath slow) more than a feeling; honest might feel wobbly in the tummy but always lands softer than a lie; and fair does not always mean the same — sometimes fair means the little one goes first.',
 why:'These four hold up every future classroom, team and friendship. Preschool teachers name them daily; this lesson makes sure your child arrives already speaking the language.',
 dev:'Delay of gratification (the famous waiting skill), moral development (honesty, fairness), courtesy as social lubricant, and rule-thinking — the first steps of understanding that rules protect everyone.',
 vocab:'patient, honest, polite, fair, please, thank you, truth, turn',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four character words — each one a superpower you can practise."\n2) Meet the panels: the calm closed eyes are PATIENT, the hand-on-heart girl is HONEST, the folded-hands boy is POLITE, the chin-pondering girl is thinking about FAIR.\n3) Practise the waiting body: timer for one minute, everyone waits together — hands quiet, breath slow. That is patience, felt.\n4) Run one honest moment: ask a question with an honest answer built in ("was the broccoli nice?") and receive the true answer warmly — truth-telling must stay safe to practise.\n5) Close with the fair-shares game below and the takeaway: "character is just four words, practised."',
 say:'"There are four words that make you the kind of person people love to play with. Patient — your body can wait, even when your inside voice is yelling NOW. Honest — your mouth tells the truth, even about the broken cup, because people can trust your words. Polite — please, thank you, excuse me: small words that open big doors. And fair — everybody gets a turn, everybody gets a share, and the little ones sometimes get to go first. That is not rules. That is character — and it fits inside a four-year-old just fine."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the character words: Patient, a boy with closed eyes waiting calmly; Honest, a girl with a hand on her heart; Polite, a boy with hands folded; and Fair, a girl pondering with a finger to her chin — fairness shown as thinking, because fair takes thought.',
 ask:'"What is the patient body doing?" "Why is honest hard sometimes?" "What are the three polite words?" "What does fair mean for the little ones?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "I waited the WHOLE minute!" "It was me who spilled it" (brave honesty) or the negotiator: "that is not fair — she had two!"',
 real:'Character moments arrive hourly: the biscuit tin on the counter (honest), the post-office queue (patient), the grandparent call (polite), the last two biscuits (fair). Quietly name them — "that was patient" — and the words collect real evidence.',
 play:'Fair Shares Café: one bowl of snacks, and your child is the server who must make it fair for everyone at the table — counting out equal shares, adjusting for the baby\u2019s small plate ("fair does not always mean identical"). Pay the server in thank-yous — polite word practise built into the payout.',
 tips:'Catch the truth gently: when your child confesses, thank the honesty FIRST, address the spill SECOND — children who are punished for confessing learn to hide, and the honest mouth closes. And let waiting practice be tiny and frequent: one minute a day beats one heroic hour at the supermarket.',
 check:'Ask: "Which word is the true mouth? What are the polite words? Does fair always mean identical?" Honest, please-thank-you-excuse-me, no — three answers and the character words are on duty.'},
'notice-understand-comfort-encourage':{
 title:'The Notice, Understand, Comfort & Encourage Lesson',one:'The four empathy steps — spot the feeling, get it, soften it, cheer the next try.',
 seoTitle:'Empathy Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Notice, Understand, Comfort, Encourage (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four empathy steps — notice, understand, comfort, encourage — with a rescue-mission game that grows kind, emotionally intelligent kids.',
 intro:'This lesson teaches empathy as a walkable path with four steps — because noticing someone\u2019s feelings is a skill, and like every skill it can be practised by a four-year-old.',
 what:'Notice, understand, comfort and encourage are the four empathy steps: notice catches the feeling on someone\u2019s face, understand works out why, comfort softens the hurt and encourage hands them courage for the next try.',
 mean:'Empathy is usually described as a feeling, but for children it is a procedure — four small moves in order. Children who know the moves run them on friends, pets and parents, and the running changes them.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: feelings show on FACES and BODIES (the noticing); the best question is "are you… because…?" (the understanding); and comfort can be quiet — a sit-together is a full sentence.',
 why:'This card is the class\u2019s kindness engine: every later people-word — support, care, comfort, include — uses these four steps. Run them here on toys and teddies first, where mistakes are free.',
 dev:'Perspective-taking (the cognitive heart of empathy), emotion recognition in others, helping behaviour, and emotional first aid — the child-sized version of what nurses call a good bedside manner.',
 vocab:'notice, understand, comfort, encourage, empathy, feelings, help',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four steps for helping a heart."\n2) Walk the panels: the hand-raised boy NOTICES, the chin-thinking girl UNDERSTANDS, the heart-hugging boy COMFORTS, the fist-up girl ENCOURAGES.\n3) Drill the sequence on a teddy with a problem (lost button, scary dream): your child runs all four steps out loud — "I notice you look sad… do you feel sad because the button is gone?… come here, I will sit with you… you can find it, I will help."\n4) Swap: the grown-up runs the steps for YOUR child\u2019s small real wobble, naming each step as you take it.\n5) Close with the rescue game below and the takeaway: "first the eyes, then the question, then the hug, then the cheer."',
 say:'"When someone is sad, our heart wants to help — but HOW? Four steps. First, notice: look at their face. Sad eyebrows? Dropped shoulders? Second, understand: ask gently — are you sad because…? Third, comfort: a hug, a hand, or just sitting close — comfort can be quiet. Fourth, encourage: when they are ready, the cheer — try again, I am right here. Eyes, question, hug, cheer. That is how you help a heart — and hearts remember everyone who helped."',
 notice:'On this card four panels show the steps: Notice, an attentive boy with his hand raised; Understand, a girl nodding with a finger to her chin; Comfort, a boy hugging a pink heart; and Encourage, a girl cheering with a fist up — the helping sequence exactly as a child runs it.',
 ask:'"What is step one — eyes or hugs?" "What question helps us understand?" "Can comfort be quiet?" "What does encourage sound like?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "I noticed my friend was sad!" "Are you sad because…?" (the full formula) or the tender one: "I sat with him until he felt better."',
 real:'Rescue missions appear all day: a sibling\u2019s dropped ice cream, a friend\u2019s skinned knee at the park, the dog hiding from the vacuum, a parent losing their keys. Run the four steps on small scale emergencies — the practice is the point, not the fix.',
 play:'Feelings Rescue Patrol: one stuffed animal per round "needs help" (a broken wheel, a lost spot, a scary noise). Your child is the rescue patrol and must run all four steps before the rescue is complete — the animal\u2019s face must go from sad to cheered. Four rescues, four full sequences, one empathic pro.',
 tips:'Resist fixing fast — the understand step needs the question, not the solution, and children skip it because grown-ups model skipping it. Slow down deliberately: "I notice your face looks disappointed… was it because…?" And praise the noticing loudest of all: eyes that catch feelings are the rarest part.',
 check:'Ask: "What comes after noticing? Can comfort be quiet? What does encourage do?" Understanding, yes, cheers — three answers and the empathy steps are in service.'},
'wait-ask-listen-carefully-say-sorry':{
 title:'The Wait, Ask, Listen Carefully & Say Sorry Lesson',one:'The four smooth-words of playdates — wait before grabbing, ask before taking, listen before answering, say sorry when it wobbles.',
 seoTitle:'Manners Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Wait, Ask, Listen Carefully, Say Sorry (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four smooth words — wait, ask, listen carefully, say sorry — with a role-play that rehearses the asking and the mending before the real playdate.',
 intro:'This lesson is the playdate survival kit: the four words that keep toys shared, games fair and friendships intact when the wobbles come.',
 what:'Wait, ask, listen carefully and say sorry are the four smooth words: wait keeps hands still, ask turns grabbing into asking, listen carefully honours what people say and say sorry repairs the moments when hands moved too fast.',
 mean:'Most toy conflicts are the same accident in different costumes — hands faster than words. These four words slow the hands down: the pause where a friendship can be asked for instead of grabbed.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: WAIT is the guard at the gate (hands still, one breath); ASK is the key ("may I have a turn?" works on almost every door); and SORRY is the repair kit — real, brief, and followed by changed hands.',
 why:'Preschool is four words away from smooth: the children who can wait, ask, listen and repair are the ones teachers trust and children include. This lesson installs the whole kit at home, where the mistakes are cheap.',
 dev:'Impulse control (the wait), social negotiation (the ask), active listening, and accountability — the sorry that means something because it is modelled, never forced.',
 vocab:'wait, ask, listen carefully, say sorry, may I, turns, gentle hands',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four words that make playdates smooth."\n2) Read the panels: still hands WAIT, the raised hand ASKS, the ear-hand LISTENS CAREFULLY, the clasped-hands girl has a SORRY ready.\n3) Rehearse the ask with the toy game below — grabbing never wins in the rehearsal, asking always does.\n4) Run the listen test: give a two-step instruction ("get the ball, then the cup") and let careful listening earn the cheer.\n5) Close with the repair rehearsal and the takeaway: "wait, ask, listen — and when it goes wrong, the sorry is always ready."',
 say:'"Here is how hands and words work together. Wait — when you want something someone has, your hands freeze and your one breath happens. Ask — then come the words: may I have a turn? It is amazing how often the answer is yes when the asking is nice. Listen carefully — when someone answers, or explains the rules, your ears take it all in before your mouth starts. And say sorry — when a hand moves faster than the words, sorry is the word that fixes it. Small word, big magic: sorry means I know I got it wrong, and I will do the asking next time."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the smooth words: Wait, a boy standing calmly with hands still; Ask, a girl raising her hand; Listen Carefully, a boy with a hand to his ear; and Say Sorry, a girl with clasped hands and a shy smile — each word shown at the moment it is needed.',
 ask:'"What do our hands do while we wait?" "What words do we use instead of grabbing?" "What do we do after sorry?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "May I have a turn — please?" (the jackpot) "I waited for the swing!" or the repaired one: "I said sorry and we played again."',
 real:'The asking life is everywhere: a sibling\u2019s toy, the shared crayons, the question for the librarian, the turn on the digger at the sandpit. Each ask is a rep — and the grown-up who models "may I borrow you for a hug?" is teaching without teaching.',
 play:'Grabbing Never Wins: roll a ball back and forth with one rule — the roller must ASK before rolling ("may I roll it to you?") and the catcher must WAIT with still hands until the ask is finished. Every grab (yours too, playfully) restarts the round. The asking habit forms in about four rounds and generalises overnight.',
 tips:'The forced sorry teaches nothing but the flinch — instead, move close, speak low, and offer the words to borrow: "would you like to say sorry? here, I will help you start." And when YOUR hand grabs (the phone snatched from small hands, for instance), say the sorry yourself: the asymmetry is the lesson.',
 check:'Ask: "What do we do instead of grabbing? What do our ears do? What word repairs a mistake?" Ask, listen carefully, sorry — three answers and the playdates run smoother by the weekend.'},
'welcome-include-cooperate-teamwork':{
 title:'The Welcome, Include, Cooperate & Teamwork Lesson',one:'The four group words — hello at the door, room in the circle, a part for everyone and the feeling when it works.',
 seoTitle:'Teamwork Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Welcome, Include, Cooperate, Teamwork (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four group words — welcome, include, cooperate and teamwork — with a fort-building mission that needs every hand in the family.',
 intro:'This lesson takes kindness public: the four words that turn a group of children into a team, starting with the door (welcome) and ending with the cheer (teamwork).',
 what:'Welcome, include, cooperate and teamwork are the four group words: welcome greets the newcomer, include opens the circle wider, cooperate gives everyone a part and teamwork is the warm feeling when all the parts work.',
 mean:'Classrooms and playgrounds run on these four words — the child who can welcome, include and cooperate becomes a social hub, and social hubs are looked up to, not left out.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: welcome is a HELLO plus a smile (it takes ten seconds); include means scanning for the one standing alone (the advanced move); and cooperate means everybody does a PART — nobody does everything.',
 why:'This card turns all the private kindness of the earlier cards outward. Kindness to a friend is love; kindness to a stranger is leadership — and four-year-olds can lead.',
 dev:'Social inclusion behaviours, cooperative play (the developmental step beyond parallel play), task division, and group belonging — the feeling of being part of a we.',
 vocab:'welcome, include, cooperate, teamwork, together, part, group',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four words that build a team."\n2) Read the panels: the waving boy WELCOMES, the open-armed girl INCLUDES, the ready boy is set to COOPERATE, and the two children arm in arm are TEAMWORK.\n3) Rehearse the hello: practise three welcomes — big wave, quiet smile, silly bow — all three count.\n4) Play the inclusion scan: "look around the playground in your mind — who is standing alone? What could we say?"\n5) Close with the fort mission below and the takeaway: "many hands, one team — and everybody does a part."',
 say:'"Groups are made of words. Welcome — hi, come in, we are glad you came. Include — you can play too; there is always room in a kind circle. Cooperate — we each take a part: you hold, I build, she finds the pieces. And teamwork — that is the feeling when it all works, the one that makes you shout WE DID IT with your whole body. Here is a secret: the strongest kids in any classroom are not the loudest — they are the ones who say you can play too."',
 notice:'On this card four panels build the team: Welcome, a waving boy; Include, a girl with her arms open wide; Cooperate, a smiling boy ready to pitch in; and Teamwork, two children with their arms around each other — the group words drawn at playground size.',
 ask:'"What does welcome sound like?" "What could we say to someone playing alone?" "What does cooperate mean — everyone does everything, or everyone does a part?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "You can play too!" "I will hold it, you build" or the proud one: "we did TEAMWORK!"',
 real:'Group moments fill the week: the new child at swimming lessons, the cousin visit, the puzzle that needs four hands, the cooking where one stirs and one pours. Name the roles out loud — "you welcome, I wash, she stirs — teamwork!" — and the words become reflexes.',
 play:'The Four-Hands Fort: a mission only possible as a team — a sofa-cushion fort that needs one holder, one builder, one fetcher and one inspector (rotate roles every five minutes). Halfway through, a stuffed animal arrives "new" and must be WELCOMED and INCLUDED with a job. Finish with the team cheer and the debrief: which word did the work?',
 tips:'Assign inclusion as a job, not a virtue: "you are our Welcomer today — watch for anyone who needs a hello." Jobs feel doable where values feel vague. And when the squabbles come mid-build (they will), that is cooperate practice, not failure — teams negotiate.',
 check:'Ask: "Which word opens the circle? Which word gives everyone a part? What do we shout when it works?" Include, cooperate, teamwork — three answers and the team words are live.'},
'hopeful-safe-problem-solver-kind-heart':{
 title:'The Hopeful, Safe, Problem Solver & Kind Heart Lesson',one:'Four inner-strength words — the look-forward feeling, the settled feeling, the fixing feeling and the engine underneath.',
 seoTitle:'Resilience Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Hopeful, Safe, Problem Solver, Kind Heart (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach four inner-strength words — hopeful, safe, problem solver, kind heart — with a silly-fixes brainstorm that builds resilience and flexible thinking.',
 intro:'This lesson installs the inner furniture: four words that describe what a strong day looks like on the inside, where nobody can see it but everybody feels it.',
 what:'Hopeful, safe, problem solver and kind heart are the inner-strength words: hopeful looks forward, safe settles down, problem solver looks for fixes and kind heart is the engine all three run on.',
 mean:'Resilience is not toughness — it is the combination of hope (this will get better), safety (I am okay right now) and problem-solving (and here is what we try). Kindness is the fuel, because strong people who are also kind are the ones others lean on.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: hopeful is a feeling you can PRACTICE ("name one good thing coming"); safe is the feeling of NOW — nothing hurts this minute; and problem solver is a POSE and a question — hands on hips, what could we try?',
 why:'This card is the deep pantry of the class: when the big feelings come — and they will — these four words are the shelf the calm-down skills sit on. Children who feel safe and hopeful try again; children who try again learn everything.',
 dev:'Optimism as a practicable habit, felt safety (the nervous system\u2019s home base), flexible problem-solving (generating options, not just one), and character integration — strength and kindness running together.',
 vocab:'hopeful, safe, problem solver, kind heart, strong, try, fix',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four words for strong days."\n2) Read the panels: the cheering boy is HOPEFUL, the content girl is SAFE, the notebook boy is the PROBLEM SOLVER, the heart-hugging girl has the KIND HEART.\n3) Practice hope: "name one thing you are excited about tomorrow" — hope is a muscle and this is its lift.\n4) Strike the problem-solver pose together: hands on hips, chin up — then pose the problem and brainstorm two SILLY fixes and one real one (silly fixes are allowed because they make the thinking playful).\n5) Close with the takeaway: "strong and kind is the whole job — and you are already both."',
 say:'"Inside every strong day are four words. Hopeful — I believe good things are coming, and I can name one: tomorrow we bake. Safe — right now, this minute, nothing here can hurt me; my body hears that and settles. Problem solver — when something goes wrong, I do not melt, I POSE (hands on hips!) and ask: what could we try? And kind heart — underneath all of it, the warm engine that does the fixing gently. Strong on the outside, kind on the inside — that is the whole secret, and it fits on one card."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the strength: Hopeful, a boy looking up with a bright cheer; Safe, a girl with closed eyes and a content smile; Problem Solver, a boy thinking over an open notebook with a pencil; and Kind Heart, a girl hugging a big pink heart — strength drawn soft, the way it feels from inside.',
 ask:'"What is one good thing coming tomorrow?" "How does safe feel in your body?" "What does the problem solver say when something breaks?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "I feel safe with you." "What could we TRY?" (the pose, unprompted) or the hopeful one: "maybe the rain will stop by park time."',
 real:'Inner-strength moments hide in ordinary hours: the plant on the windowsill growing (hopeful), the quiet before sleep (safe), the ripped book page taped together (problem solver), the sibling comforted (kind heart). Point at the taped page: "that is what a problem solver did there."',
 play:'Silly Fixes First: pose a small real problem (the tower keeps falling, the cup is too full) and race to list fixes — but the first TWO must be ridiculous (bananas! a robot! a big pillow!). Silly opens the thinking door; the third fix is usually the real one, thought of by a child who is now laughing instead of stuck.',
 tips:'Safety is built by predictability more than words — the same bedtime steps, the same goodbye ritual; the felt sense of safe comes from days that rhyme. And when the real fix fails, normalise it: "that fix did not work — good thinking though. What else could we try?" Problem solvers keep their tries.',
 check:'Ask: "What does the problem solver ask? Which feeling means this minute is okay? What runs underneath it all?" What could we try, safe, kind heart — three answers and the inner furniture is in place.'},
'joyful-tired-confused-curious':{
 title:'The Joyful, Tired, Confused & Curious Lesson',one:'The four feelings of a full day — from arm-throwing joy to a yawn, a shrug and a leaning-in question.',
 seoTitle:'Feelings Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Joyful, Tired, Confused, Curious (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four day-feelings — joyful, tired, confused and curious — with a day-in-four acting game, growing emotion vocabulary from breakfast to bedtime.',
 intro:'This lesson walks one whole day of feelings: the morning joy, the afternoon yawn, the homework shrug and the question that turns not-knowing into wanting-to-know.',
 what:'Joyful, tired, confused and curious are the four feelings of a full day: joyful is happiness with the whole body, tired is the yawn at the end of the tank, confused is not-getting-it-yet and curious is the lean-in that fixes confused.',
 mean:'Confused and curious are the same face one second apart — teach them side by side and not-knowing stops being shameful. It becomes the front door of curiosity, which is the feeling all learning runs on.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: joyful is loud-body happy and it is ALLOWED; tired is not boring, it is information (the body asking for the bed); and confused is just curious that has not started yet — the shrug and the lean-in are neighbours.',
 why:'These four cover the honest range of a real day — most feelings days are not one feeling but a parade of them. Naming the parade teaches the deepest lesson: feelings change, all day long, and that is normal.',
 dev:'Emotion granularity across a day, energy awareness (reading tiredness before meltdowns), learning attitudes (confused-to-curious reframe), and the meta-skill of noticing that feelings shift.',
 vocab:'joyful, tired, confused, curious, day, learn, question',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "One whole day of feelings."\n2) Act the panels: arms-up laughing JOYFUL, the yawn with the floating Zz TIRED, the head-scratch CONFUSED, the finger-to-chin lean-in CURIOUS.\n3) Tell the day in four: the birthday morning (joyful), after the party (tired), the new puzzle (confused), then the leaning-in (curious) — and the puzzle gets finished.\n4) Play the day-in-four game below with YOUR real day — grown-ups act too, tired included.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "feelings change all day — and confused is just curious, warming up."',
 say:'"A whole day is a parade of feelings. Morning might bring joyful — the whole-body happy, arms up, maybe even a happy shout. By afternoon, tired yawns in — that is not naughty, that is your body asking for rest. New things bring confused — the shrug that says I do not get it YET. But look at confused\u2019s neighbour: curious — the lean-in, the finger on the chin, the wanting to know. They are the same face one second apart! Confused becomes curious the moment you lean in. And curious is the feeling that learns everything."',
 notice:'On this card four panels walk the day: Joyful, a boy laughing with his arms thrown up; Tired, a yawning girl with a floating Zz; Confused, a boy scratching his head; and Curious, a girl with a finger to her chin — the parade drawn honestly, yawn and all.',
 ask:'"What does joyful do with its arms?" "Is tired naughty, or is it information?" "What is the difference between confused and curious?" "Which feeling did today start with?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Joyful is happy with jumping!" "I am tired-informationed" (close enough) or the reframe: "I am curious, not confused."',
 real:'The parade marches daily: the jump-into-your-arms morning (joyful), the 4pm slump (tired), the new rules of a new game (confused), the but-why streak (curious, gloriously). Name the weather as it changes — "oh, the tired just arrived" — and your child learns to read their own forecast.',
 play:'Day-in-Four Theatre: at dinner, everyone acts one moment of their day as one of the four feelings — no words allowed, faces and bodies only — and the rest of the family guesses. Grown-ups go first, honestly including a tired. Round two: which feeling do you want MORE of tomorrow, and how could we get it?',
 tips:'Honour the tired instead of fighting it — a child who can say "I am too tired to behave well" gets a cuddle; a child who melts gets a consequence, and both were the same feeling. And feed the curious deliberately: answer the but-why with "great question — how could we find out?" and confused never gets to feel like failure.',
 check:'Ask: "Which feeling throws its arms up? What does confused become when you lean in? Is tired allowed?" Joyful, curious, yes — three answers and the day\u2019s parade is named.'},
'scared-brave-shy-confident':{
 title:'The Scared, Brave, Shy & Confident Lesson',one:'The four courage feelings — the held breath, the fists anyway, the hiding hair and the crossed-arms smile.',
 seoTitle:'Feelings Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Scared, Brave, Shy and Confident (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four courage feelings — scared, brave, shy and confident — with a feeling-map that makes fear speakable and shyness respectable.',
 intro:'This lesson takes the courage feelings out of the whisper-zone: scared and shy get names and respect, brave and confident get honest definitions — and the family maps where each one lives.',
 what:'Scared, brave, shy and confident are the courage feelings: scared wants to hide, brave goes ahead anyway, shy warms up slowly around new people and confident stands tall in its own skin.',
 mean:'Brave is not the opposite of scared — it is scared that went anyway. And shy is not a flaw: it is a warming-up speed. Children who learn both facts stop apologising for who they are and start choosing who they are being.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: scared is a body alarm (fast heart, held breath) and alarms are information; brave is small and repeatable (one step, then another); and shy children are not broken — many scientists and writers describe themselves as shy.',
 why:'The courage feelings are the ones children hide — which makes them the ones most worth naming together. This lesson gives permission for all four, and the permission is what builds the confidence.',
 dev:'Fear literacy (naming instead of hiding), courage as behaviour, temperament respect (shy is a speed, not a defect), and self-confidence built on honest self-knowledge rather than empty praise.',
 vocab:'scared, brave, shy, confident, courage, alarm, warm up',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The four courage feelings — all of them allowed."\n2) Read the panels: the held-breath boy is SCARED, the fists-ready boy is BRAVE, the girl peeking from her hair is SHY, the crossed-arms girl is CONFIDENT.\n3) Draw the feeling map below: which things are scary-things, which are shy-things, which feel confident? The grown-up fills one in too.\n4) Tell one brave-but-small story: the first swim lesson, the lost-then-found moment — brave at four is small and that is correct.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "brave is scared that went ahead anyway — and shy is just a warming-up speed."',
 say:'"Everyone — every person, every grown-up — feels scared sometimes. Scared is your body\u2019s alarm doing its job: heart fast, breath held, feet saying hide. Brave is not the opposite of scared. Brave is scared that decided to go one step anyway — and then another. Shy is the feeling around new people: quiet first, warm later — that is a speed, not a problem; some of the world\u2019s best listeners are shy. And confident is the tall feeling when your own skin fits: arms crossed, smile up. All four feelings live in everyone. Even me. Especially me."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the courage feelings: Scared, a boy with worried hands near his face; Brave, a boy standing tall with fists ready; Shy, a girl peeking out from behind her hair; and Confident, a girl with crossed arms and a proud smile — none of them mocked, all of them drawn kindly.',
 ask:'"What does scared do to the body?" "What is brave — never scared, or scared and going anyway?" "Is shy a problem or a speed?" "Where do you feel confident?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Brave means do it anyway!" "I am shy at first" (the honest self-knowledge) or the question: "were YOU ever scared?"',
 real:'Courage weather arrives daily: the big dog behind the fence, the new Sunday class, the haircut, the dark corridor to the bathroom. Map them lightly in passing — "new places make you shy first, then confident, remember?" — and the map becomes a compass.',
 play:'The Feeling Map: a big sheet of paper, four zones drawn — scared-things, brave-things, shy-things, confident-things. The family fills it with real items: thunder (scared), the tall slide (brave), parties at first (shy), dancing in the kitchen (confident). The grown-up\u2019s honest entries — "the phone makes me shy!" — are the most valuable ones on the page. Hang it; add to it; watch zones move over the year.',
 tips:'Never call a shy child shy in front of them as an apology — "she is shy" becomes a costume they wear for you. Instead narrate the speed: "she warms up first, then the fun starts." And when scared arrives, do not argue the fear away — get curious about it: "what is the alarm saying? Is it a tiger-sized problem or a mouse-sized one?"',
 check:'Ask: "What is brave really? Is shy a problem or a speed? Name one brave thing from this week." Scared-and-going-anyway, a speed, (their story) — three answers and the courage feelings are out of the whisper-zone.'},
'share-give-care-thank':{
 title:'The Share, Give, Care & Thank Lesson',one:'The four giving words — splitting it, handing it over, holding someone close and closing the circle.',
 seoTitle:'Giving Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Share, Give, Care and Thank (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four giving words — share, give, care and thank — with a giving-circle game that practises every kind of generosity in one sitting.',
 intro:'This lesson maps the four shapes of generosity — because giving is bigger than sharing, and every child is generous in at least one shape already.',
 what:'Share splits something so two can use it, give hands it over for keeps, care holds someone close and keeps them warm, and thank closes the circle by naming what was received.',
 mean:'Children who hate sharing a treasured toy often give drawings joyfully or care for babies fiercely — generosity is a shape-shifter. Four shapes on one card means every child finds theirs and gets named for it.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: share means BOTH keep using it (the toy comes back); give means it changes owners (and that is a big, brave kind of giving); and care is giving with your whole body — hugs, blankets, band-aids, patience.',
 why:'This card deepens the early kindness words into a full giving vocabulary — the same words families, classrooms and eventually workplaces run on, practised here at snack time and teddy-bear scale.',
 dev:'Generosity in multiple forms (splitting, transferring, nurturing), gratitude expression, nurturing behaviour (care is the prototype of parenting), and the social warmth of a well-closed circle.',
 vocab:'share, give, care, thank, generous, kindness, circle',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four shapes of giving."\n2) Read the panels: the two children SHARE one teddy, the boy GIVES a wrapped gift, the grown-up CARES for a small child, the boy THANKS with hands on his heart.\n3) Sort giving: hold up scenarios — "two kids, one truck: share or give?" "a friend is cold: which word?" "a birthday present: which word?"\n4) Run the giving circle below — one tiny act of each shape, in one sitting.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "giving has four shapes, and you are already good at at least one."',
 say:'"Giving is bigger than one word — it has four shapes. Share: we both use it, and it comes back — the ultimate two-players game. Give: here, it is yours now, for keeps — the biggest kind of giving, and it feels strangely wonderful. Care: giving with your whole body — the blanket for the shivering teddy, the band-aid for the knee, the hug that fixes more than it should. And thank: the word that closes every circle — I received your kindness and I noticed. Four shapes, one heart. Which shape are you best at?"',
 notice:'On this card four panels show giving\u2019s shapes: Share, a boy and a girl sharing a teddy bear; Give, a boy handing a wrapped gift to a friend; Care, a grown-up hugging a small child close; and Thank, a boy with both hands on his heart — the whole circle of generosity at child scale.',
 ask:'"What is the difference between share and give?" "What does care look like with a teddy?" "What does thank do to the circle?" "Which giving shape do you love most?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Sharing means it comes back!" "Here, it is yours" (a real give) or the nurturer: "my teddy needed care."',
 real:'Giving shapes stack through the week: the snack split between siblings (share), the flowers picked for the neighbour (give), the sick-day blanket runs (care), the thank-you for the library story (thank). Name the shape each time — "that was a real give!" — and the vocabulary multiplies the generosity.',
 play:'The Giving Circle: five minutes, four shapes. Share one snack between plates. Give one thing to the donation bag (chosen by the child — the choosing is the lesson). Care for one patient: teddy gets the blanket and the check-up. Thank one person out loud, specifically — "thank you for drawing with me." Ring an imaginary bell between shapes; the ceremony is what makes it stick.',
 tips:'Never steal a give — when your child hands over their biscuit, accept it with the full ceremony or they learn giving lands flat. And protect the treasured-thing rule: sharing is practised on the ordinary toys; the one beloved bear is allowed to stay off-rotation while generosity grows on everything else.',
 check:'Ask: "Which giving word means it comes back? Which one means for keeps? Which one uses hugs and blankets?" Share, give, care — three answers and every shape of their generosity has a name.'},
'be-kind-helpful-patient-honest':{
 title:'The Be Kind, Helpful, Patient & Honest Lesson',one:'The four be-words — values shrunk to things a four-year-old can actually do before dinner.',
 seoTitle:'Character Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Be Kind, Helpful, Patient, Honest (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four be-words — be kind, be helpful, be patient, be honest — with missions that turn values into doable jobs, building character through action.',
 intro:'This lesson turns four big values into four small jobs — because at four, character is not discussed, it is DISPATCHED: one kindness mission, one helper job, one waiting moment, one honest report.',
 what:'Be kind, be helpful, be patient and be honest are the be-words: kindness done on purpose, help spotted and given, patience practised in real queues, honesty told in real wobbly moments.',
 mean:'"Be" words work differently from other words — they name the child, not just the act. A child who hears "you were patient today" starts collecting evidence of being a patient person, and identity drives behaviour far more than rules do.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: be-words are JOBS, not grades (small, today-sized, doable); helpful means SEEING the job (eyes first, hands second); and honest earns more than it costs — the true answer is always received warmly in this house.',
 why:'This card sits after the feelings vocabulary because character needs a base: children who can name feelings find it far easier to be kind, patient and honest — the be-words are where the vocabulary becomes visible.',
 dev:'Prosocial identity formation, helping behaviour (initiated, not assigned), delay of gratification in natural settings, and moral courage — the honest report is a four-year-old\u2019s first ethics exam.',
 vocab:'be kind, helpful, patient, honest, mission, job, true',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four be-words — each one a job you can do today."\n2) Read the panels: heart-shaped hands BE KIND, the teddy packed into a box BE HELPFUL, folded hands BE PATIENT, the hand-on-chest girl BE HONEST.\n3) Dispatch the missions: one kindness mission (child\u2019s choice), one helper job (socks match, cushions returned), one waiting moment (the oven timer), one honest moment (a question answered true).\n4) Debrief each with the word, not the lecture: "you WERE patient. You WERE honest."\n5) Close with the takeaway: "be-words are not rules — they are costumes you can try on, and they start to fit."',
 say:'"Here are the four be-words, and each one is a job for today. Be kind — one kindness mission, your choice: a drawing delivered, a sibling helped, a flower found. Be helpful — eyes open for a job nobody asked you to do; helping first is the whole trick. Be patient — one waiting moment, felt on purpose: hands quiet, breath slow, timer ticking. Be honest — when the question comes, tell it true, even the wobbly parts; in this family, true answers are always safe. Do the four jobs and something funny happens: they stop being jobs. They start being you."',
 notice:'On this card four panels give the be-words bodies: Be Kind, a boy shaping a heart with his hands; Be Helpful, a girl tucking a teddy bear into a box; Be Patient, a boy with folded hands and closed eyes; and Be Honest, a girl with a hand on her chest — values drawn as actions, not posters.',
 ask:'"What is your kindness mission today?" "How do we spot a job nobody asked for?" "What does the patient body do?" "Is the true answer always safe here?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "I WAS patient!" "I helped without being asked" or the brave one: "it was me who knocked it over."',
 real:'The be-words have job sites everywhere: the neighbours\u2019 bins (helpful), the waiting room (patient), the who-drew-on-the-wall tribunal (honest), the sibling\u2019s fallen tower (kind). Dispatch small and often — four tiny jobs a day build more character than any poster on the wall.',
 play:'Mission Control: each morning, pick ONE be-word as the day\u2019s secret mission and write it where the child can see. At dinner, the family reports mission evidence — and everyone hunts for the mission in EACH OTHER. A week later, the child picks the missions for the grown-ups. Watching a four-year-old assign "be patient" to a parent is character education at its finest.',
 tips:'Praise in the past tense of identity — "you were so patient waiting for the glue" — not "good boy": the first builds a self-image, the second builds a compliance habit. And when the honest report is hard to hear, thank the honesty before addressing the mess; the order of those two sentences decides whether honesty survives to age five.',
 check:'Ask: "Which be-word spots jobs nobody asked for? Which one tells it true? What do be-words become after enough practice?" Helpful, honest, you — three answers and the be-words are on the job.'},
'forgive-accept-respect-include':{
 title:'The Forgive, Accept, Respect & Include Lesson',one:'The four deeper friendship words — start fresh, let be, treat as valuable, open the circle.',
 seoTitle:'Friendship Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Forgive, Accept, Respect, Include (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four deeper friendship words — forgive, accept, respect and include — with an open-circle game that makes group kindness practisable.',
 intro:'This lesson is the deeper course of friendship: four words for the wobbles and the differences that every real group of children eventually meets.',
 what:'Forgive starts the game again after a fight, accept lets people be different without losing friendship, respect treats every person as valuable, and include opens the circle so nobody stands outside it.',
 mean:'These four words make groups kind — a playdate of two only needs sorry and share, but a classroom of twenty runs on forgive, accept, respect and include. They are the civilisation words of childhood.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: forgive means the fight does not follow you into the next game; accept means different is allowed (different foods, different families, different speeds); and respect is NOT hitting, NOT mocking, and DO listening — even when you disagree.',
 why:'The earlier friendship card taught the ladder (smile, care, forgive, friend); this one deepens it for real groups — the messy, wonderful, many-person world of preschool, where differences and conflicts arrive together.',
 dev:'Conflict recovery at group scale, acceptance of difference (the root of inclusion later), respect as behaviour (not feeling), and circle-keeping — the social architecture of making room.',
 vocab:'forgive, accept, respect, include, different, circle, fresh start',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four deeper friendship words."\n2) Read the panels: clasped hands FORGIVE, the open-armed girl ACCEPTS, the thoughtful boy RESPECTS, the welcoming girl INCLUDES.\n3) Play the open-circle game below — the circle literally opens wider every time someone is let in.\n4) Sort the differences: "some friends eat different food, some families have one parent, some kids talk later — is different okay?" (Always yes; different is the point.)\n5) Close with the takeaway: "forgive the past, accept the difference, respect the person, include the everyone."',
 say:'"Friendship in a big group needs four strong words. Forgive — when someone messes up, we start fresh; the fight does not follow us to the next game. Accept — people are different, and different is allowed: different snacks, different homes, different speeds. Respect — I treat you like you matter: I do not hit, I do not mock, I DO listen. And include — the circle opens; there is always room for one more. Those are the words that make a group kind. Any group. Even this family."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the deep friendship words: Forgive, a boy with clasped hands and calm eyes; Accept, a girl with her arms open wide; Respect, a thoughtful boy with a hand to his chin; and Include, a girl welcoming with open arms — the group values drawn at child scale.',
 ask:'"What does forgive do with yesterday\u2019s fight?" "Is different okay?" "What does respect NOT do?" "What does include do with the circle?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Forgive means fresh start!" "Different is okay" or the observer: "we let him play even though he is little."',
 real:'Deep friendship moments recur all week: the sibling truce after the storm (forgive), the friend who eats different food (accept), the quiet kid who hates being teased (respect), the newcomer at music class (include). Each one is a rep — name the word where the moment lives.',
 play:'The Open-Circle Game: four or more toys (or people) form a tight circle playing a simple ball-pass. A fifth toy approaches — shy, outside. The circle\u2019s job: make the circle BIGGER, not tighter — include the newcomer, teach the game, pass to them second. Play three rounds; by the last one, your child is running the welcoming without a script.',
 tips:'Respect is best taught by what it refuses to do — "in this family we do not mock, even when it is funny" — and what it always does: listen to the other side first. And separate forgive from consequences: "I forgive you, AND the rule still stands" is a sentence children need to hear; forgiveness is not amnesia.',
 check:'Ask: "Which word starts things fresh? Which word makes room for different? Which one opens the circle?" Forgive, accept, include — three answers and the deeper friendship words are at work.'},
'pause-breathe-deeply-think-choose-kindness':{
 title:'The Pause, Breathe Deeply, Think & Choose Kindness Lesson',one:'The four-step pause — the gap between the spark and the flame, where a child gets to choose.',
 seoTitle:'Calm-Down Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Pause, Breathe, Think, Choose Kindness (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four-step pause — pause, breathe deeply, think, choose kindness — the self-regulation sequence that turns big feelings into kind choices.',
 intro:'This lesson teaches the single most valuable move in the whole class: the pause that fits between a feeling and a reaction, in four steps a four-year-old can run.',
 what:'Pause, breathe deeply, think and choose kindness are the four steps of the pause: freeze the moment, slow the body, see what is happening and then — the best part — choose what happens next.',
 mean:'Between the spark and the flame there is a gap. Self-regulation is nothing more mysterious than a gap wide enough to choose in — and this card widens it with two words (pause, breathe) and two moves (think, choose).',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: the pause is a FREEZE (everything stops, including words); the breath is the remote control (slow breath, slow body); and the last step is a real CHOICE — nobody makes you kind; you get to pick it.',
 why:'Every calm-down tool in the class — the courage ladder, the count to ten, the reset — is a variation of this pause. Learn it here, at calm-home scale, and it will be there in the storm.',
 dev:'Self-regulation (the executive-function showpiece), impulse control, metacognition (thinking about what is happening), and moral agency — the child as the chooser of their own next move.',
 vocab:'pause, breathe deeply, think, choose, kindness, freeze, gap',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "This is the pause — the space where YOU choose."\n2) Run it small: someone says PAUSE — everyone freezes mid-motion (freeze dance style). Hold three seconds. Then one deep breath together: in through the nose, out slow.\n3) Add the think: "what is happening right now?" One sentence. Then the fourth step, spoken like a secret power: "and I choose kindness."\n4) Rehearse it on a pretend spark: "suppose your brother knocked your tower — PAUSE. Breathe. Think: he did not mean it. Choose: shall we build it again together?"\n5) Close with the freeze-dance game below and the takeaway: "the pause is yours; the choice is yours; the power is yours."',
 say:'"Here is the strongest four-step move I know. One: PAUSE — everything stops, like a statue, even the angry words. Two: breathe deeply — in through the nose, out slow, and your body hears it and starts to settle. Three: think — what is actually happening? (He did not mean it. The game broke. I am tired.) Four — and this is the magic: CHOOSE. Nobody makes you yell and nobody makes you kind. You choose. And when you choose kindness, you are the strongest person in the room. The pause is where the power lives."',
 notice:'On this card four panels run the sequence: Pause, a still boy with closed eyes; Breathe Deeply, a girl with a hand on her chest mid-breath; Think, a boy with a finger to his temple; and Choose Kindness, a girl hugging a pink heart — the whole gap between spark and choice, drawn in four frames.',
 ask:'"What is step one — freeze or fix?" "What does the deep breath do to the body?" "Who chooses the last step?" "What did YOU choose recently?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Pause means everything stops!" "I choose kindness" (wield it gently when they do) or the honest one: "I forgot to pause and then I remembered."',
 real:'Real sparks arrive hourly: the knocked tower, the taken crayon, the wrong cup, the end of screen time. Run the pause at each one — often silently, hand on chest as the signal — and the gap becomes a habit their body knows even when their feelings are loud.',
 play:'Freeze-Dance with a Choice: music dances, then stops — PAUSE! Everyone freezes; one deep breath together; the leader asks "how do we choose to play the next round?" and offers two kind options (gentle hands? new game? swap partners?). The children pick — the choosing is the lesson, dressed as a dance game. Rotate the leader every round.',
 tips:'Practise the pause on calm days, never in the storm — the drill must live somewhere safe before it can work somewhere hard. And use the silent signal (hand on your own chest) instead of shouting PAUSE at a boiling child: the gesture says we do this together, which is the only version that teaches.',
 check:'Ask: "What stops first? What does the slow breath do? Who makes the last choice?" Pause, settles the body, me — three answers and the pause belongs to your child.'},
'proud-disappointed-grateful-peaceful':{
 title:'The Proud, Disappointed, Grateful & Peaceful Lesson',one:'The wins-and-setbacks feelings — the tall smile, the drooped hope, the warm thanks and the quiet settle.',
 seoTitle:'Feelings Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Proud, Disappointed, Grateful, Peaceful (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the wins-and-setbacks feelings — proud, disappointed, grateful and peaceful — with a dinner ritual that builds resilience and gratitude together.',
 intro:'This lesson covers the two feelings that follow every win (proud, grateful) and the two that follow every setback and bedtime (disappointed, peaceful) — the honest after-taste of a real day.',
 what:'Proud is the tall smile of a thing done, disappointed is the droop when the hoped-for thing does not happen, grateful is the warm thanks for what did, and peaceful is the quiet settle at the end of it all.',
 mean:'Disappointed is the feeling nobody teaches and every child needs — small daily doses (the sold-out cookies) build the muscle for the big doses later. And grateful is its partner: the same day can hold both, and naming both is resilience.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: proud is celebrated out loud (tall back, told people); disappointed is allowed to be sad for a while — it shrinks when it is spoken; and peaceful is a feeling you can VISIT — the last minute before sleep is its front door.',
 why:'This card balances the class: after the big feelings and the courage words, here is the honest ledger of winning and wanting — and the quiet that closes every day regardless of the score.',
 dev:'Achievement emotions (pride as motivation), disappointment tolerance (small-dose resilience practice), gratitude habit (the strongest single happiness habit in the research), and down-shift skills for the evening settle.',
 vocab:'proud, disappointed, grateful, peaceful, win, setback, settle',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The after-feelings — what follows the wins and the wobbles."\n2) Read the panels: crossed arms and a tall smile are PROUD, the drooped girl with a hand to her cheek is DISAPPOINTED, clasped hands are GRATEFUL, the softly smiling girl is PEACEFUL.\n3) Tell one story of each: the finished puzzle (proud), the rained-off park trip (disappointed), the unexpected biscuit (grateful), the last story before sleep (peaceful).\n4) Play the dinner ritual below — one proud, one disappointed, one grateful each, grown-ups first.\n5) Close with one peaceful minute and the takeaway: "same day, all four feelings — and that is a good day."',
 say:'"Every day hands us four after-feelings. Proud — when you do something hard, your back straightens all by itself; say it out loud: I did it. Disappointed — when the thing you hoped for does not happen, the shoulders droop; that is allowed, and it gets smaller when you say it: I really wanted the park. Grateful — the warm feeling when something good arrives, expected or not; naming it doubles it. And peaceful — the quiet at the end, no wins needed, just breath and blanket. Proud, disappointed, grateful, peaceful: a whole honest day, four names."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the after-feelings: Proud, a boy with crossed arms and a tall smile; Disappointed, a drooping girl with a hand to her cheek; Grateful, a boy with hands clasped at his chest; and Peaceful, a girl with arms open and a soft smile — the honest ledger of a day, drawn gently.',
 ask:'"What does proud do to your back?" "Is disappointed allowed?" "What does grateful do to a good thing?" "When does peaceful usually visit?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "I am SO proud of my tower." "I am disappointed about the park" (the full word, working) or the nightly one: "I am grateful for… you."',
 real:'The after-feelings register daily: the mastered zip (proud), the closed playground (disappointed), the found parking spot (grateful), the lamp-off hush (peaceful). Grown-ups who say their own out loud — "honestly, I am disappointed the picnic got rained off; let us find one grateful thing anyway" — teach all four in one sentence.',
 play:'Proud-and-Grateful at Dinner: three questions, every night, everyone answers (grown-ups first, honestly): one proud thing, one disappointed thing, one grateful thing. Then thirty seconds of peaceful together — lights low, one slow breath. Two minutes total; the ritual does more for resilience and gratitude than any poster ever printed.',
 tips:'Do not fix the disappointed — rush in with a replacement treat and the feeling never gets its reps. Sit in it briefly, name it, trust it to shrink. And keep pride about the DOING ("you practised until it worked") rather than the being ("you are so clever") — process-pride survives setbacks, person-pride does not.',
 check:'Ask: "Which feeling straightens the back? Which one is allowed to droop a while? What closes the day?" Proud, disappointed, peaceful — three answers and the after-feelings are on the table.'},
'worried-relieved-jealous-embarrassed':{
 title:'The Worried, Relieved, Jealous & Embarrassed Lesson',one:'The four tricky feelings — the what-if, the phew, the wish-it-were-mine and the wish-they-would-stop-looking.',
 seoTitle:'Feelings Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Worried, Relieved, Jealous, Embarrassed (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four tricky feelings — worried, relieved, jealous and embarrassed — with grown-up stories that make the hardest feelings speakable.',
 intro:'This lesson names the four feelings children hide: the worry that whispers, the relief that lands, the jealousy that burns and the embarrassment that blushes — all of them normal, all of them nameable.',
 what:'Worried is a what-if that will not stop, relieved is the phew when the worry ends, jealous is the hot wish for what someone else has, and embarrassed is the blush when everyone is looking.',
 mean:'Jealous and embarrassed are the loneliest feelings of preschool — children carry them secretly, sure they are the only ones. The cure is not a lecture; it is a grown-up saying "I felt that too, last Tuesday."',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: worried is the brain trying to protect you (it just over-protects); jealous means you noticed something you want — and wants can be talked about; and embarrassed shrinks fastest when someone kind stays normal beside you.',
 why:'This is the class\u2019s bravest card: the feelings children least want to admit are the ones most worth naming together. A child who can say "I am jealous" at four is learning to bring shadows into the light — for life.',
 dev:'Worry literacy (naming the what-ifs), relief recognition (marking safety), envy vocabulary (jealousy as information about wants), and shame prevention — the early inoculation that embarrassment is universal and survivable.',
 vocab:'worried, relieved, jealous, embarrassed, what if, tricky feelings, normal',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The four tricky feelings — everyone has them."\n2) Read the panels: the finger-to-mouth boy is WORRIED, the eyes-closed relieved girl just felt the phew, the crossed-arms boy is JEALOUS, the blushing hunched girl is EMBARRASSED.\n3) Tell the grown-up stories below — one real, light story per feeling, told with a smile not a shadow.\n4) Practise the relief sound: "phew" — said out loud with wiped brows; relief deserves its own word.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "tricky feelings are not bad feelings — they are just shy. Named, they behave."',
 say:'"Some feelings play hide-and-seek. Worried is the what-if feeling: what if the dark, what if the first day, what if nobody plays with me — your brain is trying to protect you; it just shouts sometimes. Relieved is what lands after: phew — the worry was bigger than the thing. Jealous is the hot one: my friend has it and I want it TOO — that feeling is just information about a want, and wants can be said out loud. And embarrassed is the blush: everyone looking, wishing they would stop. Here is the secret about all four: every single person feels them — me, you, the neighbours, everyone. Named out loud, they get small. That is the whole trick."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the tricky feelings: Worried, a boy with a finger to his mouth; Relieved, a girl with closed eyes and hands clasped in relief; Jealous, a sulking boy with crossed arms; and Embarrassed, a blushing girl hunched with clasped hands — every one drawn kindly, none of them caricatured.',
 ask:'"What does worried whisper?" "What sound does relieved make?" "What is jealous telling you?" "What helps embarrassed most?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Worried is a what-if!" "Phew!" (marking their own relief) or the confession: "I felt jealous when she had the bigger cake."',
 real:'Tricky feelings visit weekly: the stormy night (worried), the found toy (relieved), the sibling\u2019s bigger slice (jealous), the potty accident at a friend\u2019s house (embarrassed). Meet each one at its address — name it lightly, tell yours, move on. The lightness is the lesson.',
 play:'Grown-Up Story Swap: one feeling a night, four nights. The grown-up tells one true, small story — a worry that turned out fine, a jealous moment, an embarrassing tumble — told lightly, laughed WITH not at. Then the child may swap one of their own or just listen. No lessons attached; the stories do the teaching, and the safety does the rest.',
 tips:'Never say "you have nothing to be embarrassed about" — to a blushing child that is one more person looking. Instead, stay normal: sit beside, carry on gently, let the moment pass in good company. And treat jealousy as a wants-report, not a character flaw: "you noticed you wanted that. Wants can be spoken. Want to tell me about it?"',
 check:'Ask: "Which feeling says what-if? Which one says phew? Are the tricky feelings normal?" Worried, relieved, yes — three answers and the shyest feelings are out of hiding.'},
'notice-name-share-feelings-ask-for-help':{
 title:'The Notice, Name, Share Feelings & Ask for Help Lesson',one:'The four-step check-in — notice the weather inside, name it, share it, and call for help when it is too big.',
 seoTitle:'Feelings Check-In Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Notice, Name, Share, Ask for Help (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four-step feelings check-in — notice, name it, share feelings, ask for help — the daily habit that turns tantrums into sentences.',
 intro:'This lesson condenses the whole class into one habit: the check-in — four steps that turn a storm inside into a sentence outside.',
 what:'Notice, name, share feelings and ask for help are the four steps of the check-in: notice what is happening inside, name the feeling, share it out loud and — when it is too big to carry alone — call for help.',
 mean:'A feeling that gets noticed, named and shared almost never has to come out as a tantrum. The check-in is not one skill among many — it is the delivery system that carries every other word of this class to where it is needed.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: feelings announce themselves in the BODY first (chest, tummy, hot face) — the noticing is a body scan; the naming can start fuzzy ("something big") and sharpen later; and ask-for-help is not the last resort — it is step four of the design.',
 why:'This is the engine card of the class: every feeling word learned so far rides on the check-in. Children who run these four steps daily need fewer meltdowns, recover faster and ask for help sooner — the trifecta.',
 dev:'Interoception (reading internal signals), emotion labelling under real conditions, self-disclosure (sharing is a social skill too), and help-seeking — the skill teachers and parents wish every child had installed.',
 vocab:'notice, name, share feelings, ask for help, check-in, weather inside, too big',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The check-in — four steps from storm to sentence."\n2) Read the panels: the hand-raised boy NOTICES, the chin-finger girl NAMEs IT, the open-hand boy SHAREs FEELINGS, the clasped-hands girl is ready to ASK FOR HELP.\n3) Run the body scan: close eyes, one breath, "where do you feel something? Chest? Tummy? Face?" — noticing is scanning, not guessing.\n4) Practise the fuzzy-to-sharp move: "something big… is it angry? worried? excited? — let us try names until one fits."\n5) Close with the daily ritual below and the takeaway: "notice, name, share — and the fourth step is always there: ask for help."',
 say:'"Here is the check-in — the strongest habit on the whole feelings path. Step one, NOTICE: put a hand on your chest and ask, what is happening inside? Something is always happening. Step two, NAME it: start fuzzy — something big — then try names until one fits: worried? frustrated? excited? Step three, SHARE it: say it out loud to someone safe — I feel worried. Step four, the safety net: if the feeling is too big to carry alone, ASK FOR HELP — can you help me? That is not giving up. That is exactly what step four is for. Four steps, two minutes, and the storm has somewhere to go."',
 notice:'On this card four panels run the check-in: Notice Feelings, a boy with his hand raised mid-notice; Name It, a girl with a finger to her chin; Share Feelings, a boy telling with an open hand; and Ask for Help, a girl with hands clasped, ready to ask — the delivery system of the whole class, in four frames.',
 ask:'"Where do you feel feelings in your body?" "What if the name will not come — can we start fuzzy?" "What do we do when the feeling is too big?" "Who are your help-people?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "My tummy feels something big!" "I feel worried" (the whole system, working) or the request: "can you help me with my big feeling?"',
 real:'Check-in weather happens twice a day at minimum: breakfast ("what is the weather inside this morning?") and bedtime ("what was the biggest feeling today?"). Build the ritual at those two anchors and the steps carry themselves — storms get reported in sentences by the second week.',
 play:'The Feelings Weather Report: a pretend microphone, and everyone takes turns broadcasting their inside weather — "this morning in my chest: cloudy with a chance of grumpy, clearing after breakfast." Grown-ups go first with a real one. The child\u2019s forecast may be silly; the noticing underneath it is not. Two minutes, twice a day, habit installed.',
 tips:'Accept fuzzy names happily — "a big something" is a valid step two; precision arrives with practice. And make the help-people explicit: name three (and include one outside the house — a teacher, a grandparent), because a child who knows exactly WHO to ask asks sooner. Ask-for-help praised loudly is a meltdowns-later investment.',
 check:'Ask: "Where does the check-in start — the body or the mouth? What if the name will not come? What is step four?" Body, start fuzzy, ask for help — three answers and the check-in is running.'},
'say-hello-make-space-be-curious-ask-nicely':{
 title:'The Say Hello, Make Space, Be Curious & Ask Nicely Lesson',one:'The four joining-in words — the hello, the scoot-over, the question about you and the may-I.',
 seoTitle:'Social Skills Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Say Hello, Make Space, Be Curious, Ask Nicely (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four joining-in words — say hello, make space, be curious, ask nicely — rehearsed on toys first, so the real playground feels already played.',
 intro:'This lesson is the joining-in kit: four words that open doors, save seats, start friendships and get things gently — rehearsed at home so the playground feels already played.',
 what:'Say hello opens the door, make space saves the seat, be curious asks about the other person, and ask nicely gets things without grabbing — the four smallest words with the biggest social mileage.',
 mean:'Most friendships at four start with logistics, not chemistry: someone said hello, someone scooted over, someone asked a question. Children who own the logistics get more at-bats — and friendships are a numbers game at first.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: hello comes in all sizes (wave, smile, whisper — all count); making space is a BODY move (scoot, slide, make room) that says you belong here without a word; and be curious flips the spotlight — the fastest way to be interesting is to be interested.',
 why:'This card turns the class\u2019s inner words outward at playground scale: the feeling words become door-words. It sits right before the cooperation card because joining is step one; building together is step two.',
 dev:'Social initiation (the hello), spatial inclusion (making room), conversational curiosity (asking about others), and polite requesting — the may-I that replaces the grab everywhere it is installed.',
 vocab:'say hello, make space, be curious, ask nicely, join in, may I, scoot',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four words that get you into the game."\n2) Read the panels: the waving boy SAYs HELLO, the hand-on-chest girl MAKEs SPACE, the wondering boy BEs CURIOUS, the folded-hands girl ASKs NICELY.\n3) Rehearse on toys (below) before people — two stuffed animals run the whole sequence: hello, scoot, "what do you like?", "may I borrow the crayon?"\n4) Practise three hellos in the mirror: big wave, quiet smile, silly eyebrow — all valid.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "the game does not start until someone says hello — be the someone."',
 say:'"Here is how you get into any game, anywhere. One: say hello — big wave, small smile, even a whisper; a hello is a hello, and it opens the door. Two: make space — scoot over, slide along, hold the blanket open; making room says you belong here louder than words. Three: be curious — ask the new friend something: what do you like? what can that truck do? Being interested in someone is the fastest way to become their friend. Four: ask nicely — may I have a turn, please? Works on toys, swings, crayons and almost every grown-up alive. Four small words. Whole friendships start with less."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the joining-in words: Say Hello, a waving boy; Make Space, a girl with a hand on her chest stepping aside; Be Curious, a boy wondering with a finger to his chin; and Ask Nicely, a girl asking with hands folded together — the kit drawn at playground-door size.',
 ask:'"What are the three sizes of hello?" "What does make space look like on a bench?" "What question could you ask a new friend?" "What works better than grabbing?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Hello — want to play?" (the whole door, three words) "here is space for you!" or the report: "I asked nicely and she said YES."',
 real:'Doors open all week: the new child at story time, the cousin arriving, the shared sandbox, the school open day. Run the kit in miniature on the way in — "shall we say hello first, or make space first?" — and let your child pick the door they open.',
 play:'The Toy Playdate: two stuffed animals meet at a tiny bench. Your child runs both roles (or you take one): hello (different voices are encouraged), make space on the bench, be curious — "what do YOU like?" — and ask nicely for the shared crayon. Swap roles, run it again, then retire the toys and deploy the same four moves at the real playground this week.',
 tips:'Resist scripting the friendship — hand over the four moves and let your child\u2019s version be weird if it wants; a robot-dance hello that lands is worth more than a polished one that never leaves the house. And when the ask-nicely fails (it will sometimes), debrief the Ask, not the rejection: "good asking. Sometimes the answer is not yet — want to try again after her turn?"',
 check:'Ask: "What opens the door? What does make space say without words? What works better than grabbing?" Hello, you belong here, asking nicely — three answers and the joining-in kit is packed.'},
'share-space-work-together-take-turns-cheer-others':{
 title:'The Share the Space, Work Together, Take Turns & Cheer Others Lesson',one:'The four playdate words — room for two, many hands, my-go-your-go and the cheer that is not for me.',
 seoTitle:'Cooperation Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Share Space, Work Together, Take Turns, Cheer Others (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four playdate words — share the space, work together, take turns, cheer others — with a one-block-each build that runs all four in one game.',
 intro:'This lesson runs the full playdate: four words that handle the crowd — sharing the floor, building together, queuing for the good parts and cheering whoever wins the moment.',
 what:'Share the space, work together, take turns and cheer others are the four playdate words: room for two at the table, many hands on the build, my-go-your-go for the good parts and the cheer that points at someone else.',
 mean:'Cheering others is the advanced course: a four-year-old who claps for a friend\u2019s success has learned the opposite of jealousy — the feeling that quietly decides how good group life will be for everyone including the cheerer.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: sharing SPACE is easier and different from sharing things (room at the table, room in the game); taking turns means the wait has an END (my-go-your-go, then it rolls back); and cheering is a skill — specific beats generic ("you stacked the BIG one!" outcheers "yay").',
 why:'This card is the group-practice run for everything the class has taught: feelings named, pause learned, kindness chosen — here it all meets other children, where the weather is real.',
 dev:'Cooperative and turn-based play, spatial generosity, waiting with an end in sight, and other-praising — the celebration of someone else\u2019s success, which research keeps linking to lasting friendships.',
 vocab:'share the space, work together, take turns, cheer others, my go, your go, yay',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four words that run a playdate."\n2) Read the panels: two children SHARE THE SPACE over one book, two more WORK TOGETHER with a high-five, the waiting boy TAKEs TURNS, the thumbs-up girl CHEERs OTHERS.\n3) Name the difference: sharing things (the truck) versus sharing SPACE (the rug, the table, the game) — space is easier to start with.\n4) Run the one-block-each build below — all four words in one game.\n5) Close with the cheer practice: everyone invents one specific cheer for someone else\u2019s real success today, and delivers it.',
 say:'"Playdates run on four words. Share the space — the rug, the table, the game: there is room for both of us, scoot in. Work together — many hands build faster: you hold it, I stack it, we both cheer the tower. Take turns — the good parts are worth waiting for, and the wait has an end: my go, your go, my go again. And cheer others — when someone else does something great, YOUR thumbs go up: you stacked the BIG one! Here is the secret about cheering: it feels even better than being cheered. That is not a joke — try it and see."',
 notice:'On this card four panels run the playdate: Share the Space, a boy and a girl reading one book together; Work Together, two children high-fiving; Take Turns, a patient boy waiting with a hand on his cheek; and Cheer Others, a girl with both thumbs up — group life drawn at carpet-time scale.',
 ask:'"What is the difference between sharing a toy and sharing the space?" "Does the turn come back?" "What makes a cheer GREAT — yay, or you stacked the big one?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "There is room for both of us!" "My go — then your go!" or the advanced one: "GOOD STACKING, Mia!"',
 real:'Playdate mechanics repeat weekly: the crowded craft table (share the space), the bakery-shop game (work together), the single swing (take turns), the sibling\u2019s first pedal strokes (cheer others). Each is a live rep — narrate the word as it happens and the mechanics become manners.',
 play:'One-Block-Each Tower: build a block tower where the ONLY rule is one block each, then pass — no exceptions, grown-ups included. Every placement earns a specific cheer from the previous builder ("tall one! perfect corner!"). The tower falls eventually (they always do) — rebuild rule stands, and so does the cheering. Five minutes, all four words, one laughing family.',
 tips:'Cheering needs models more than instructions — a household that says "your brother zipped it himself — tell him what you noticed!" raises children who look for material to cheer. And keep turns SHORT at four: the wait is the exercise, so make the reps frequent and tiny; long turns teach only endurance.',
 check:'Ask: "What do we share besides toys? What do we say while waiting? Who is the cheer for?" The space, my-go-your-go, someone else — three answers and the playdate runs itself.'},
'kind-choice-good-friend-try-your-best-grow-together':{
 title:'The Kind Choice, Good Friend, Try Your Best & Grow Together Lesson',one:'The four growing words — the choice, the showing-up, the honest effort and the getting-bigger side by side.',
 seoTitle:'Growth Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Kind Choice, Good Friend, Try Your Best, Grow Together (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four growing words — kind choice, good friend, try your best, grow together — with a bedtime rewind that turns every day into growth evidence.',
 intro:'This lesson hands your family growth language: four words that measure the day by growing, not by winning — and a bedtime rewind that collects the evidence.',
 what:'Kind choice, good friend, try your best and grow together are the growing words: the moment you picked kind over easy, the showing-up for someone, the honest effort (not the perfect result) and the getting-bigger that happens side by side.',
 mean:'Growth language beats cleverness language in every study that compares them: children praised for trying choose harder puzzles; children praised for smart choose easier ones. This card installs the first vocabulary at family scale.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: kind choice means the pick was yours (nobody made you — that is what makes it strong); try your best is about the TRYING, and best changes day to day (tired-best counts); and grow together means the growing is a WE project — family, friends, everyone.',
 why:'This card names the arc of the whole class: feelings learned, kindness chosen, and now — growing. It sits before the graduation cards because it gives the family the ruler to measure everything after.',
 dev:'Growth mindset language (process over person), prosocial choice-making (agency), effort attribution, and collective identity — the WE that grows, which is belonging\u2019s twin.',
 vocab:'kind choice, good friend, try your best, grow together, effort, choice, growing',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The growing words — how we measure a day."\n2) Read the panels: the heart-hugging girl made a KIND CHOICE, the open-armed boy is a GOOD FRIEND, the fist-up boy TRYs HIS BEST, the sprout-cupping girl shows GROW TOGETHER — the only panel holding something alive and small.\n3) Ask the rewind questions (below) at bedtime — growth language installed in ninety seconds.\n4) Retire the word smart for a week: replace every "so clever" with "you worked until it worked" — feel the difference in the child\u2019s posture.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "we do not measure days by winning — we measure them by growing."',
 say:'"In this family we have growing words. Kind choice — when you picked kind even though easy was right there: that was YOURS, you chose it. Good friend — when you showed up: sat with the sad one, waited for the slow one, shared the last one. Try your best — not perfect, just your all; and best is allowed to be tired on Tuesdays. Grow together — because nobody grows alone: we get bigger side by side, you and me and everyone in this house. Those are our words. Not how much, not how fast, not who won — did we grow? We did. Every day, a little."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the growing words: Kind Choice, a girl hugging a heart with closed eyes; Good Friend, a boy with his arms open wide; Try Your Best, a determined boy with a fist up; and Grow Together, a girl cupping a tiny green sprout in her hands — the only card in the class holding a living thing, because that is the point.',
 ask:'"What was today\u2019s kind choice?" "Who did you show up for?" "What did you try your best at — even a tired best?" "What should WE grow tomorrow?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "I made a kind choice!" "I showed up for Sam" or the planner: "tomorrow we grow a bigger tower."',
 real:'Growth evidence accumulates quietly: the stair climbed alone (try your best), the truce offered (kind choice), the friend waited for (good friend), the family plant watered (grow together). Name the evidence in passing — the child collects the identity; you just point.',
 play:'The Bedtime Rewind: three questions in the dark, every night: what was today\u2019s kind choice? who was a good friend today (anyone, including you, including a story character)? what did you try your best at? Then one grow-together plan for tomorrow — one seed, chosen together. Ninety seconds, no stickers, no charts — the remembering IS the growing.',
 tips:'Guard the word best — best is not perfection, and a tired child\u2019s best is still best; say so out loud or the word starts lying. And when the rewind finds nothing one night (some days are like that), the grown-up donates one: "I saw you pick up the baby\u2019s cup — that was a kind choice." The ruler always finds something when someone else holds it kindly.',
 check:'Ask: "What makes a kind choice strong? What is try-your-best about — the result or the trying? Who grows alone?" That it is chosen, the trying, nobody — three answers and the family has its ruler.'},
'calm-down-count-to-ten-use-your-words-take-a-break':{
 title:'The Calm Down, Count to Ten, Use Your Words & Take a Break Lesson',one:'The four emergency moves — slow the body, count the breath, say the words and, when the storm is too big, step out and come back.',
 seoTitle:'Calm-Down Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Count to Ten, Use Words, Take a Break (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four real calm-down moves — calm down, count to ten, use your words, take a break — drilled on quiet days so they work on stormy ones.',
 intro:'This lesson is the emergency services: four moves for the stormy moments, drilled here on quiet days so they are there on loud ones.',
 what:'Calm down slows the body, count to ten gives the storm something to do, use your words sends the feeling out as a sentence and take a break is the honoured exit — step away, settle, come back when the storm is smaller.',
 mean:'Calm-down skills are rehearsed, not wished for — that is why calm-down corners exist in every early-years classroom. A child who has PRACTISED the four moves on a happy Tuesday can find them on a furious Thursday.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: the body calms FIRST (soft shoulders, slow breath — words come after); counting is not magic, it is a JOB for the busy brain (ten fingers, ten breaths); and take a break is not a punishment — it is strategy, and coming back is part of the move.',
 why:'Every class needs its fire drill. The feelings vocabulary, the pause, the courage ladder — all of it gets stress-tested here, and the four moves on this card are what remain standing in a real storm.',
 dev:'Down-regulation under arousal (the hardest self-regulation tier), sequencing (body before words), the counting strategy (attention redirection), and honouring limits — knowing when to step away is wisdom, not defeat.',
 vocab:'calm down, count to ten, use your words, take a break, storm, settle, come back',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The four storm moves — for the days that go sideways."\n2) Read the panels: the folded-hands girl CALMs DOWN, the finger-counting boy COUNTS TO TEN, the open-hand boy USEs HIS WORDS, the beanbag child TAKEs A BREAK.\n3) Drill the body first: shake out the arms, then soft shoulders, slow breath — the body leads, the words follow.\n4) Count together: ten slow fingers, one number per breath — feel the speed drop around seven.\n5) Close with the calm-corner build below and the takeaway: "calm the body, busy the brain, say the words — and the break is always allowed."',
 say:'"Some moments bring a storm — and here are the four moves. Calm down: your body goes first — shoulders soft, feet still, one slow breath; the body is the rudder. Count to ten: one finger per breath, one… two… three… by seven the storm has usually lost interest. Use your words: now, with a calmer mouth — I am angry BECAUSE…; words let the feeling out the front door. And take a break: if the storm is still too big, you may ALWAYS step away — to the cushion, the corner, my lap — and come back when it is smaller. That is not quitting. That is strategy. Firefighters drill on quiet days too."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the storm moves: Calm Down, a serene girl with folded hands and closed eyes; Count to Ten, a boy counting on a raised finger; Use Your Words, a boy speaking with an open hand; and Take a Break, a child resting curled on a beanbag — the emergency kit drawn comfortably, beanbag included.',
 ask:'"What calms first — the body or the mouth? What job does counting give the brain? Is take-a-break a punishment or a strategy?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Body first!" "One… two… three…" (mid-storm — the sound of it working) or the strategic one: "I need a break."',
 real:'Storms arrive on schedule: the tower collapse, the wrong-coloured cup, the leaving-the-park, the bedtime that came too soon. Walk the four moves through each real storm, slowly, beside your child — and on the quiet days, drill them with giggles so the moves feel like old friends, not strangers.',
 play:'Calm-Corner Dress Rehearsal: on a sunny afternoon, let your child choose the spot, name it (the Harbour? the Cozy Corner?) and stock the basket — one book, one bear. Then run the fire drill nobody needs: visit together for one slow breath while everything is completely fine, and leave while it is still nice. A corner rehearsed in sunshine works in rain; a corner met for the first time mid-storm just feels like timeout.',
 tips:'The grown-up\u2019s calm is the curriculum: your slowed voice, lowered body and one audible breath of your own teach more than any script — co-regulation first, self-regulation later, that is the developmental order. And honour the break without ceremony: no sighing, no lesson on the way out; the break that works is the one that is just allowed.',
 check:'Ask: "What calms first? What is the counting FOR? Is take-a-break allowed?" The body, a job for the busy brain, always — three answers and the storm moves are drilled.'},
'i-feel-i-need-i-want-i-choose':{
 title:'The I Feel, I Need, I Want & I Choose Lesson',one:'The four I-statements — the weather inside, the helping request, the honest wish and the owned decision.',
 seoTitle:'Speaking-Up Lesson for Ages 4–5 — I Feel, I Need, I Want, I Choose (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four I-statements — I feel, I need, I want, I choose — giving a small person a big, peaceful voice at home and preschool.',
 intro:'This lesson hands your child the four sentences that start with I — a voice that speaks feelings, needs, wishes and decisions without grabbing, hitting or whining.',
 what:'I feel names the weather inside, I need asks for what would help, I want speaks the honest wish and I choose owns the decision — four sentence-starters that carry a whole voice.',
 mean:'I-statements are the peaceful alternative to grabbing, hitting and whining — the same message with the fighting stripped out. A child with I-words stops needing behaviour as a language.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: I FEEL is about inside weather (not "you made me" — just "I feel"); I NEED and I WANT are different (needs help, wants wishes — both allowed, said differently); and I CHOOSE is the proudest one — it means the decision is yours to own.',
 why:'This card equips the check-in with sentence-starters: noticing and naming feelings (earlier cards) become SPEAKABLE sentences here. It is also the quiet alternative to every power struggle in the day.',
 dev:'Assertive communication (the healthy middle between passive and aggressive), needs-versus-wants differentiation, ownership language, and emotional expression in sentence form — the vocabulary of the whole class, armed with grammar.',
 vocab:'I feel, I need, I want, I choose, I-statement, my voice, own it',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "Four sentences that start with I — your biggest voice."\n2) Read the panels: the chin-thinking girl says I FEEL, the open-armed boy says I NEED, the plain-spoken boy says I WANT, the confident girl says I CHOOSE.\n3) Model one of each, honestly: "I feel tired. I need five quiet minutes. I want tea. I choose the sofa."\n4) Play the I-statement dinner game below — one sentence per starter, everyone, grown-ups included.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "sentences that start with I are yours — nobody can interrupt your inside weather."',
 say:'"You have four magic sentence-starters, and every one begins with I. I feel — the inside weather: I feel sad, I feel excited, I feel that wobbly something. I need — what would help: I need a hug, I need help with the zip, I need you to listen. I want — the honest wish: I want the blue cup, I want a turn — wants are allowed; we just say them instead of grabbing them. And I choose — the proudest one: I choose this book, I choose to share. Four starters. When your words start with I, people lean in to listen — because you sound like someone who knows their own heart."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the I-statements: I Feel, a thoughtful girl with a hand to her chin; I Need, a boy explaining with open arms; I Want, a boy saying it plainly with crossed arms; and I Choose, a confident girl with a hand on her chest — a whole voice, drawn in four poses.',
 ask:'"Which starter talks about inside weather?" "What is the difference between a need and a want?" "Which starter owns the decision?" "What is YOUR I-feel right now?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "I feel… jumpy!" "I need help, please" or the owned one: "I choose the green one."',
 real:'I-statement moments appear all day: the blocked view at the fence (I need to see too), the feared haircut (I feel scared), the shared crayon (I want a turn next), the outfit standoff (I choose the dinosaur shirt). Grown-ups who use them out loud — "I need ten quiet minutes, then I am all yours" — hand over the grammar for free.',
 play:'The I-Game at Dinner: four rounds, one per starter. Round one: everyone says an I-feel (real or silly). Round two: an I-need. Round three: an I-want — no judgement, wishes are wishes. Round four: an I-choose (about something small and real: tomorrow\u2019s breakfast, the walk route). Grown-ups go first each round — especially with one real "I need five quiet minutes", which lands funnier than any lecture.',
 tips:'Coach the difference without the lecture: needs and wants both get said, but needs get met faster — "you NEED a band-aid, you WANT more pudding; the band-aid is coming, the pudding is a maybe." And when the I-feel names YOU as the villain ("I feel angry AT YOU"), accept the feeling, reroute the sentence: "I hear angry. And I feel — what else? …worried? tired? Let us start there." The I stays; the blame goes.',
 check:'Ask: "What starts every sentence today? Which one is the proud owner one? Are wants allowed?" I, I choose, yes — three answers and the small person has a big voice.'},
'listen-first-think-first-speak-kindly-solve-together':{
 title:'The Listen First, Think First, Speak Kindly & Solve Together Lesson',one:'The four peace-process steps — ears before mouth, think before speak, kind before right, together before me.',
 seoTitle:'Problem-Solving Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Listen, Think, Speak Kindly, Solve Together (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four peace steps — listen first, think first, speak kindly, solve together — the order that turns squabbles into negotiations.',
 intro:'This lesson teaches the order that settles squabbles: not who is right, but what runs first — ears, then think, then kind words, then the together-fix.',
 what:'Listen first, think first, speak kindly and solve together are the four peace steps: full ears before any mouth, a thinking beat before speaking, kind words even in disagreement, and a fix both people can live with.',
 mean:'Conflict is not the enemy of friendship — unhandled conflict is. Children who know the ORDER (ears, think, kind, together) turn squabbles into their first negotiations, and a four-year-old negotiator is a thing of beauty.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: listening goes FIRST because both sides need air (no interrupting — the hardest and highest skill); the think-beat is one breath between hearing and answering; and speaking kindly is not weak — it is how the strong actually win arguments.',
 why:'This card is where the class meets real friction: two children, one toy, both certain. The feelings words set the table; this card serves the meal — the actual mechanics of two people finding the together-fix.',
 dev:'Conflict resolution sequencing, active listening under disagreement, prosocial assertion (kind AND firm), and joint problem-solving — the together-fix as the earliest form of compromise.',
 vocab:'listen first, think first, speak kindly, solve together, both sides, fix, fair',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The four steps that settle any squabble."\n2) Read the panels: the ear-hand girl LISTENs FIRST, the chin-thinking boy THINKs FIRST, the open-hand boy SPEAKs KINDLY, the two facing children SOLVE TOGETHER.\n3) Drill the order with a prop squabble: two bears, one honey pot. You narrate each step as the bears run it — ears (each bear says what it heard the other say), think (one breath), kind (no roars), together (both bears\u2019 fix).\n4) Deploy it on the next real dispute as the narrator: you speak the step-names, the children do the steps.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "the fix is never me-win — it is us-fixed."',
 say:'"Squabbles have an order, and here it is. Listen first — ALL the way to the end of what they are saying; ears open, mouth shut, even when you already know you are right (especially then). Think first — one breath between their words and yours: what did they actually mean? Speak kindly — you can be firm AND kind at the same time; roars make ears close, kind words keep them open. Solve together — not I-win, not you-win: the fix BOTH of us can live with. Two bears, one honey pot? Listen, think, speak kindly… and split it, or take turns, or find a bear who already ate. The order does the work."',
 notice:'On this card four panels run the peace process: Listen First, a girl listening with a hand to her ear; Think First, a boy pondering with a finger to his chin; Speak Kindly, a boy talking with an open friendly hand; and Solve Together, two children talking face to face — the order drawn exactly as it should be run.',
 ask:'"What comes before the mouth?" "How long is the think-first beat?" "Can you be firm and kind at the same time?" "Whose fix is it in the end?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Ears first!" "Let me think for a second" (the beat, arriving) or the negotiated one: "we could take turns AND share the top."',
 real:'Disputes queue up daily: the swing, the front seat, the last sticker, the loudest singer. Run the order as narrator on the small ones — naming each step as it happens — and by the umpteenth small one, the children run the steps without you. Then retire gracefully to the refreshments.',
 play:'The Bear Court: a squabble is brought before the family\u2019s wisest bear (a teddy on a cushion). The bear (voiced by your child, ideally) enforces the order: each side speaks, the OTHER side must say back what it heard, one think-breath, kind words only, then the together-fix proposed by the cubs themselves. Bear adjourns with a gavel paw. Silly, sacred, and it teaches the sequence better than any diagram.',
 tips:'Narrate the steps, not the verdict: "that was listen-first… now the think-breath…" — children doing the steps need the step-names, not a judge. And when the together-fix is lopsided (always give the little one the honey), let it stand: fairness calibrates over months of practice, and a fix both AGREED to is the lesson, whatever the honey math.',
 check:'Ask: "What runs first? What sits between hearing and answering? Whose fix wins?" Ears, one breath, both of us — three answers and the peace process is in service.'},
'welcome-all-be-inclusive-share-joy-make-a-friend':{
 title:'The Welcome All, Be Inclusive, Share Joy & Make a Friend Lesson',one:'The four open-door words — everyone gets a hello, nobody plays alone, happiness given away and the friend at the end of it.',
 seoTitle:'Inclusion Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Welcome All, Be Inclusive, Share Joy, Make a Friend (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four open-door words — welcome all, be inclusive, share joy, make a friend — with an inclusion mission that grows real social courage.',
 intro:'This lesson is the open-door policy in child size: four words that make a child the one who greets, gathers, glows and — sometimes before dinner — makes a friend.',
 what:'Welcome all greets every comer, be inclusive scans for the one standing alone, share joy hands happiness over out loud, and make a friend is the day\u2019s best possible achievement.',
 mean:'Inclusion is kindness aimed at a group: the child who notices the left-out one and opens the circle is doing social leadership at four. The world runs on exactly this, and it starts here, at the sandbox.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: welcome ALL means no auditions (nobody has to be funny or fast to get a hello); be inclusive is a SCANNING skill — eyes up, notice who is alone; and joy is contagious on purpose — a shared "look at THIS!" doubles on the spot.',
 why:'This card gathers the joining-in kit (say hello, make space) and the group words (include, cooperate) into one open-door habit — the final social skill before the belonging cards close the class.',
 dev:'Social leadership (initiating inclusion), empathy in action (noticing the outsider), joy expression (shared delight as social glue), and friendship formation skills — the actual mechanics of the first friend.',
 vocab:'welcome all, be inclusive, share joy, make a friend, hello, alone, together',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The open-door words."\n2) Read the panels: the wide-armed girl WELCOMEs ALL, the heart-hugging boy BEs INCLUSIVE, the fist-up boy SHAREs JOY, the waving girl MAKEs A FRIEND.\n3) Rehearse the scan: "imagine the playground — who is standing alone? What are three things we could say or do?"\n4) Practise the joy-share: one child shows a treasure (a drawing, a shell), the family responds with real delight — joy grows when it lands.\n5) Close with the inclusion mission below and the takeaway: "the door does not open itself — someone opens it. Be the someone."',
 say:'"Here is the open-door way to play. Welcome ALL — every single person gets a hello; no tryouts, no auditions: the fast one, the slow one, the new one, the loud one. Be inclusive — keep your eyes up: if someone is standing alone, you are the one who walks over — you can play too is the strongest sentence on any playground. Share joy — when something delights you, DELIVER it: look at this! Joy given away comes back doubled; that is its whole magic. And make a friend — today, this week, at your own speed. Someone opened a door for your first friend. Now you know how the door works."',
 notice:'On this card four panels open the door: Welcome All, a girl with her arms open wide; Be Inclusive, a boy hugging a pink heart; Share Joy, a boy cheering with a fist up; and Make a Friend, a girl waving hello — the open-door policy drawn at sandbox scale.',
 ask:'"Who gets a hello under welcome all?" "What do your eyes do when you are being inclusive?" "What happens to joy when you give it away?" "Who opened a door for you once?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Nobody plays alone!" "Look what I found — come SEE!" or the mission report: "I said hi to the new girl and she likes unicorns TOO."',
 real:'Open-door moments arrive weekly: the new starter at swimming, the birthday party where someone knows nobody, the park at busy hour, the family gathering with the shy cousin. Pre-brief lightly ("want a mission or just a hello today?") and debrief gently — the door-words need reps, not pressure.',
 play:'The Inclusion Mission: once a week, one official mission — at the park, the class, the gathering, be the one who says hello to someone new or brings the standing-alone child into the game. Choose the mission together, keep it small (one hello counts), and debrief at dinner: how did it feel to open the door? The answer — usually proud, sometimes shy — is the growth, spoken aloud.',
 tips:'Let the mission be optional — inclusion chosen freely sticks; inclusion assigned sours. And when your child reports a failed hello (the other child walked off), treat it as weather, not verdict: "sometimes doors take three tries. The opening still counts." Door-openers need their courage protected more than their success rates.',
 check:'Ask: "Does welcome all have tryouts? What are your eyes doing when they are being inclusive? What happens to shared joy?" No, scanning, it doubles — three answers and the door is theirs to open.'},
'you-matter-i-am-here-you-are-safe-we-care':{
 title:'The You Matter, I Am Here, You Are Safe & We Care Lesson',one:'The four reassurance sentences — the words every child needs to hear, and to learn to say.',
 seoTitle:'Reassurance Lesson for Ages 4–5 — You Matter, I Am Here, You Are Safe, We Care (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four reassurance sentences — you matter, I am here, you are safe, we care — for hard days, goodbyes and the voice a child carries inside.',
 intro:'This lesson is the family\u2019s anchor: four sentences said on the hard days until they become the voice your child carries inside their own head.',
 what:'You matter, I am here, you are safe and we care are the four reassurance sentences: worth declared, presence promised, safety named and belonging wrapped around all of it.',
 mean:'Reassurance works by rhythm and repetition, not novelty — the SAME four sentences on every hard day become internalised. A child who has heard you-are-safe a thousand calm times can find it once in a storm.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: you matter is unconditional (not you matter WHEN you behave — just you matter); I am here is a promise about presence, not a promise about never leaving (I come back is the goodbye version); and these words are TRANSFERABLE — your child can offer them to a friend, a baby, a teddy, even you.',
 why:'This card is the class\u2019s heart: every feeling word, calm-down skill and kindness habit rests on the floor these four sentences build. It sits with the belonging cards because it IS belonging, spoken.',
 dev:'Secure attachment language, felt safety (the nervous system\u2019s home), unconditional worth (worth separate from behaviour), and comforting Others — the child as giver of the same four sentences.',
 vocab:'you matter, I am here, you are safe, we care, reassurance, come back, belong',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The four sentences — for hard days and every day."\n2) Read the panels: the clasped-hands girl says YOU MATTER, the reaching boy says I AM HERE, the peaceful boy says YOU ARE SAFE, the heart-hands girl says WE CARE.\n3) Say all four to your child, slowly, on an ordinary day — reassurance must not live only in emergencies.\n4) Teach them as GIFTS: practise offering them to a teddy after a fall ("you matter, I am here, you are safe, we care") — children give comfort brilliantly when handed the words.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "these four sentences are yours to hear, and yours to give."',
 say:'"There are four sentences I will say to you on every hard day, and plenty of easy ones. You matter — exactly as you are, on your best day and your worst, no conditions attached. I am here — right beside you; and when I must go, the promise becomes I come back, and I always do. You are safe — this minute, this room, these arms: nothing here can hurt you. And we care — the whole family, a whole row of people, all facing your direction. Say them back to me when you learn them. Give them to your teddy. Give them to a friend who falls. These words are not just for receiving — they are for carrying."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the sentences: You Matter, a girl with warm clasped hands; I Am Here, a boy reaching out a steadying arm; You Are Safe, a boy with closed eyes, calm; and We Care, a girl shaping a heart with her hands — the anchor drawn simply, the way the sentences are said.',
 ask:'"What are the four sentences?" "What does I-am-here become when I have to leave?" "Who else could we say them to?" "Do they still count on a grumpy day?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "You matter, teddy. I am here." (the whole gift, delivered) "you came back!" or the question: "say the safe one again."',
 real:'Anchor moments repeat: the nightmare at 3am (you are safe, I am here), the preschool goodbye (I come back — after lunch, I promise), the scraped knee (you matter, we care), the ordinary Tuesday for no reason at all (all four, just because). The ordinary-day repetitions are the ones that build the storm-time voice.',
 play:'Pass the Sentences: a soft ball passed around the circle (toys included) — the holder receives all four sentences from the group, then passes. Round two: the holder SAYS the four to the next holder. By round three, your child is comforting a stuffed rabbit with the full sequence, and the sentences are officially theirs — to hear, and to give.',
 tips:'Keep the sentences UNCONDITIONAL — you matter after the tantrum counts double, because that is when a child most needs to know worth is not on trial. And when you must leave, never swap I am here for silence: the goodbye ritual (I come back, I always do, watch the clock with me) is the same anchor in its travel shape.',
 check:'Ask: "Name the four sentences. What does the promise become when you leave? Who can give these words?" You-matter-I-am-here-you-are-safe-we-care, I come back, anyone — three answers and the anchor holds.'},
'keep-trying-learn-celebrate-be-yourself':{
 title:'The Keep Trying, Learn, Celebrate & Be Yourself Lesson',one:'The four confidence words — through the wobbles, from every go, for every win, in your own way.',
 seoTitle:'Confidence Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Keep Trying, Learn, Celebrate, Be Yourself (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four confidence words — keep trying, learn, celebrate, be yourself — with a family celebration ritual that cheers effort first.',
 intro:'This lesson builds the confidence shelf: four words that carry a child through the wobbles of learning anything — and one family ritual that makes sure the wins get their moment.',
 what:'Keep trying walks through the wobble, learn harvests every attempt, celebrate marks the wins (small ones loudest) and be yourself is the standing rule underneath: there is only one you, and that is the good news.',
 mean:'Celebrate is the most skipped word in child-rearing — progress that is never marked quietly stops. A family that cheers small wins out loud raises children who attempt big things, because the trying has learned it leads somewhere good.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: wobbles are PART of trying, not a verdict on you; every attempt teaches something (the learn is automatic once you look for it); and being yourself is not the last step — it is the ground all four words stand on.',
 why:'This card is the stamina of the class: feelings named, kindness chosen, skills drilled — and here is the fuel that keeps a child going when the zip sticks on the fortieth attempt. It also pre-loads the graduation: be yourself is what the whole path was for.',
 dev:'Persistence (the continue-through-difficulty muscle), learning orientation (attempt as data), celebration habits (marked progress sustains motivation), and self-acceptance — identity as the base camp for all achievement.',
 vocab:'keep trying, learn, celebrate, be yourself, wobble, win, only one you',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The confidence words."\n2) Read the panels: the fist-up girl KEEPs TRYING, the question-mark boy LEARNs, the sparkling boy CELEBRATEs, the thumbs-up winking girl BEs HERSELF.\n3) Tell the wobble story: something you failed at first, tried again, and what the second attempt taught — grown-up wobbles are the best teaching material in the house.\n4) Hold the celebration below — one HARD thing, cheered properly, effort named as the hero.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "wobble, learn, celebrate, repeat — and stay you the whole time."',
 say:'"Here are the four words that make hard things possible. Keep trying — the wobble is part of it; every climber slips, every drawer sticks, every zip fights back on the first try. Learn — every attempt teaches something, even the ones that flop; especially those. Celebrate — when something lands, MARK it: cheer, dance, tell Dad, tell the neighbour\u2019s cat; small wins counted loudly are what keep the trying coming. And be yourself — underneath everything, the standing rule: there is only one you in the whole world, and your way of doing things is allowed to be yours. Wobble, learn, celebrate — and stay you. That is the whole confidence recipe."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the confidence: Keep Trying, a girl with a determined fist up; Learn, a wondering boy with a question mark over his head; Celebrate, a boy cheering under little sparkles; and Be Yourself, a winking girl with her thumb up — effort drawn as the hero, sparkle and all.',
 ask:'"What is a wobble — a stop sign or part of trying?" "What does a flopped attempt teach?" "Which wins do we celebrate — only the big ones?" "What is something ONLY you do?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Wobbles are part of it!" "I learned the wet sponge slips" (harvested) or the self-knowledge: "I do my dancing silly and that is MY way."',
 real:'Confidence moments stack daily: the shoe attempt (forty tries), the tower collapse, the first pedal stroke, the wobbly note at singing time. Mark each with the right word — "you kept trying," "what did that teach us," "CELEBRATE!" — and the words become the child\u2019s inner soundtrack by default.',
 play:'The Hard-Thing Celebration: once a week, the family celebrates one HARD thing — confetti optional, cheering mandatory. The rule: the thing must have involved wobbles. Each person tells theirs (grown-ups first: the jammed printer counts), the effort is named as the hero ("you tried four ways!"), and the celebration is real: a dance, a dessert, a lap of the table. Effort that gets celebrated comes back for more.',
 tips:'Celebrate the specific, not the superlative — "you tried the zipper upside down AND the right way" beats "amazing!" every time; specificity proves you actually watched. And guard be-yourself from becoming a costume: the winking, thumbs-up child on the card is not a requirement — your quiet child\u2019s be-yourself might look like standing at the edge of the party, watching, until ready. That counts. That is the word working.',
 check:'Ask: "What are wobbles — part of it or a stop sign? Which wins get celebrated? What is the rule underneath all four?" Part of it, all of them (small ones loudest), be yourself — three answers and the confidence shelf is loaded.'},
'welcome-belong-trust-connect':{
 title:'The Welcome, Belong, Trust & Connect Lesson',one:'The four family words — the open door, the seat that is yours, the kept promise and the thread underneath.',
 seoTitle:'Belonging Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Welcome, Belong, Trust, Connect (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four belonging words — welcome, belong, trust and connect — with a belonging hunt that finds the child\u2019s name all over the house.',
 intro:'This lesson begins the graduation: four words about WE — the open door, the fixed spot, the kept promise and the thread that runs underneath every one of them.',
 what:'Welcome opens the door, belong finds the seat that is yours, trust keeps the promises that hold, and connect is the checking-in thread that runs underneath all three.',
 mean:'Belonging is not a mood that appears — it is built out of small fixed things: a coat hook with a name, a cup that is always theirs, a goodbye that always comes with a return. Children who can NAME their belonging carry it anywhere.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: welcome is a door you can HOLD for others now (you know how it feels to be welcomed — pass it on); belong is made of EVIDENCE (hooks, cups, seats, songs — pointable things); and trust is kept promises, small ones most of all.',
 why:'The class closes its circle here: the feelings words named the inside, the kindness words aimed outward — and the belonging words name the WE that holds it all. Everything after this card is harvest.',
 dev:'Sense of belonging (the strongest single protective factor in child development research), trust-building through reliability, family identity (the we), and welcoming agency — the child as door-holder for others.',
 vocab:'welcome, belong, trust, connect, family, promise, my spot',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The four WE words."\n2) Read the panels: the waving girl WELCOMEs, the content boy BELONGs, the hand-on-heart girl TRUSTs, the open-armed boy CONNECTs.\n3) Run the belonging hunt below — find the child\u2019s name (literally or figuratively) all over the house.\n4) Make one small trust-promise today and keep it visibly: "I said I would save you the sticker — here it is." Trust is built at sticker scale.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "welcome, belong, trust, connect — four beams, one house, and you live in it."',
 say:'"Four words hold our family up. Welcome — you are wanted here, always; the door is not locked to you, not ever. Belong — this is your hook, your cup, your seat, your bedtime song; you are not a guest here, you are a beam in the house. Trust — I keep my word to you, small word, small promises especially: the sticker saved, the park kept, the return on time. And connect — we check in, we laugh at the same silly thing, we are us. Four beams. And you — you get to hold the door for others now, because you know exactly how it feels from the inside."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the WE: Welcome, a waving girl; Belong, a content boy with hands clasped; Trust, a girl with a hand on her heart; and Connect, a boy reaching out with open arms — the family\u2019s beams, drawn warmly.',
 ask:'"Where is your spot in this house?" "What is a promise kept this week?" "What does connect look like in our family?" "Who could WE welcome this week?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "That is MY hook!" "you kept your promise!" (trust, noticed) or the pass-it-on: "can my friend come in? I will hold the door."',
 real:'Belonging evidence fills the house: the toothbrush with the favourite colour, the drawing taped at child height, the same three books at bedtime, the seat that is always theirs in the car. Point at them occasionally — "yours, yours, yours" — and the abstract word becomes a house tour.',
 play:'The Belonging Hunt: walk the house with a detective\u2019s eye and find ten pieces of belonging evidence — the hook, the cup, the shoe spot, the bedtime song, the drawing on the wall, the family photo, the garden patch, the car seat, the snack shelf, the always-yours chair. Photograph or draw the best ones into a little book titled WHERE I BELONG. Belonging, once you hunt it, turns out to have been everywhere.',
 tips:'Build trust at the smallest scale — children audit promises at four with forensic seriousness, so promise LESS and keep MORE; a kept sticker-promise outweighs a hundred lectures on reliability. And when the family welcomes a guest, hand your child the door: the welcomed child who becomes the welcomer has completed the entire lesson without noticing it was a lesson.',
 check:'Ask: "Which word means the seat is yours? What is trust made of? Who holds the door now?" Belong, kept promises, you do — three answers and the house stands.'},
'notice-pause-breathe-reset':{
 title:'The Notice, Pause, Breathe & Reset Lesson',one:'The four-step reset button — spot it coming, stop everything, one slow breath and start the moment fresh.',
 seoTitle:'Reset Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Notice, Pause, Breathe, Reset (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four-step reset — notice, pause, breathe, reset — the family word that stops a spiral without blame and restarts the moment fresh.',
 intro:'This lesson gives your family a single, blame-free power move: the RESET — four steps that stop a going-sideways moment and start it again, fresh, no guilt attached.',
 what:'Notice spots the moment tipping, pause stops everything mid-motion, breathe runs one slow breath through the group, and reset starts the bit again from the top — same game, fresh start, nobody in trouble.',
 mean:'Most meltdowns spiral because nobody has a word that stops them without blame. RESET is that word: it names the moment, not the child — the play tipped, so we reset the play. Nobody is the villain of a reset.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: the noticing is a SKILL (spotting the wobble before the crash — the raised hand, the too-loud voice); the pause includes the grown-up (everyone freezes — that is the fun and the fairness); and reset means FRESH — the last thirty seconds are not brought to the new start as evidence.',
 why:'This card is the emergency word for the whole family system: siblings\u2019 games, parent-child standoffs, the pre-dinner spiral. One word, four steps, blame-free — the single highest-leverage habit in the class.',
 dev:'Early warning detection (noticing escalation before it lands), group co-regulation (the shared pause), fresh-start thinking (separating the moment from the child), and family culture — the rituals that make a household feel safe.',
 vocab:'notice, pause, breathe, reset, fresh start, do-over, together',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The reset button — for moments going sideways."\n2) Read the panels: the hand-raised girl NOTICEs, the still boy PAUSEs, the folded-hands boy BREATHEs, the refreshed girl RESETs with a small fist.\n3) Play reset-tag below until the word itself does the work.\n4) Demo a grown-up reset: mid-fluster (spilled juice, lost keys), call RESET on yourself — hand up, freeze, one breath, "okay — fresh start." The self-reset is the strongest model in the house.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "nobody is in trouble at a reset — the moment just begins again."',
 say:'"Our family has a magic word: RESET. Here is how it works. Someone NOTICES — a hand goes up — uh oh, this is tipping over. Everyone PAUSEs — mid-motion, even grown-ups, even the dog if he will cooperate. One slow BREATHE together — in through the nose, out slow, the whole room settles an inch. And then: RESET — the game, the moment, the whole bit starts again from the top, fresh. Here is the rule that makes it magic: nobody is in trouble at a reset. Not you, not me. The moment just begins again. Watch me do it to myself sometime — grown-ups need the reset button most of all."',
 notice:'On this card four panels run the reset: Notice, an attentive girl with her hand raised; Pause, a still boy with closed eyes; Breathe, a boy with folded hands mid-breath; and Reset, a refreshed girl with a small fist up — the family\u2019s emergency word, drawn calm.',
 ask:'"What does the raised hand mean?" "Who has to pause — just kids, or everyone?" "Is anyone in trouble at a reset?" "What gets to start again?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "RESET!" (hand up, delighted) "you have to pause TOO, Daddy" or the merciful one: "we reset, nobody is in trouble."',
 real:'Reset moments repeat daily: the game that turns shouty, the dinner-table wobbles, the getting-out-the-door chaos, the sibling spiral. The word works on everyone\u2019s moments — and the day your child calls RESET on YOUR fluster, the habit is fully installed (it will happen; be ready to honour it instantly).',
 play:'Reset Tag: play any fast game — catch, tickle-monster, dance — with the family rule that anyone may shout RESET. When it is called: freeze, one breath together, then the game restarts from the top. The giggles come from the pausing mid-pounce (especially the grown-up caught mid-tickle). After a week of play-resets, the word starts working on real storms — same freeze, same breath, same fresh start, now with a purpose.',
 tips:'Honour every reset instantly — a reset that gets argued with ("we are NOT resetting, we are LEAVING") teaches the word is decoration. And keep the no-blame rule sacred: the moment anyone adds "this is your fault" to a reset, the magic dies. The fresh start IS the discipline; it needs nothing else attached.',
 check:'Ask: "Who can call reset? What happens to the last thirty seconds? Is anyone in trouble?" Anyone, left behind, nobody — three answers and the family has its button.'},
'care-support-encourage-comfort':{
 title:'The Care, Support, Encourage & Comfort Lesson',one:'The four helping words — noticing, carrying a corner, cheering the next try and sitting close.',
 seoTitle:'Caring Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Care, Support, Encourage, Comfort (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four helping words — care, support, encourage and comfort — with a care-for-the-caregiver game that lets children practise real comfort.',
 intro:'This lesson is for the days someone in the family is having a hard one: four helping words, each one a different way to be there — and a game that puts your child in the comforter\u2019s seat.',
 what:'Care notices the hard time, support carries part of the load, encourage cheers the next try and comfort sits close — a hug, a blanket, a presence, no fixing required.',
 mean:'Children are usually on the receiving end of these four words — flipping the roles is the lesson: a child invited to care for a struggling grown-up (lightly, kindly, for real) builds empathy faster than any lecture could install it.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: care starts with NOTICING (the sad face, the quiet voice — eyes first); support means carrying a CORNER of the load, not the whole thing (a cup fetched, a sock matched); and comfort does not fix — it sits with. Sitting with is a skill and it is enough.',
 why:'This card completes the empathy arc that started with notice-understand-comfort-encourage earlier in the class — now the four helping words get deployed on real family days, including the grown-ups\u2019 hard ones.',
 dev:'Nurturing behaviour (the prototype of caregiving), empathy in deployment (noticing, then acting), appropriate helping (matching the help to the need), and emotional reciprocity — the family as a two-way care system.',
 vocab:'care, support, encourage, comfort, hard day, help, sit with',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The four helping words — for someone\u2019s hard day."\n2) Read the panels: the heart-hugging girl CAREs, the hand-on-chest boy SUPPORTs, the fist-up boy ENCOURAGEs, the teddy-hugging girl COMFORTs.\n3) Sort the helps: "Dad lost his keys — care, support, encourage or comfort?" (care: notice. support: help look. encourage: you will find them! comfort: sit with the grumble.) Any order works — the sorting is the thinking.\n4) Play care-for-the-caregiver below: the grown-up has a (mildly, safely theatrical) hard day and the child runs all four.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "comfort does not fix — comfort SITS. Sitting is enough."',
 say:'"When someone is having a hard day, there are four helping words. Care — you NOTICE: your face looks sad; care starts with the eyes, always. Support — you CARRY a corner: I will hold the heavy end, I will fetch your cup, I will help look for the keys. Encourage — you CHEER the next try: you have got this; try once more — I am right here. And comfort — you SIT: a hug, a blanket, your shoulder; comfort does not fix the problem and does not have to. Sitting close IS the fix, mostly. Four words. You already have all of them in you — today we take them out and use them."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the helping words: Care, a girl hugging a pink heart; Support, a boy with a hand on his chest, ready to help; Encourage, a boy cheering with a fist up; and Comfort, a girl hugging her teddy bear — the helping set drawn at teddy-and-family scale.',
 ask:'"Which helping word starts with the eyes?" "What does support carry — the whole load or a corner?" "Does comfort have to fix?" "Who comforted YOU recently?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "I noticed you look tired!" (care, live) "I will help carry that!" or the tender one: "come here, I will sit with you."',
 real:'Hard days visit every family member: the work wobble, the bad-night\u2019s tiredness, the stubbed toe, the sad news from grandma. Let your child run the four on small scale — a cup fetched, a drawing delivered, a sit-together on the sofa — and debrief gently: "your care helped. That is a real thing you did."',
 play:'Care for the Caregiver: announce (honestly but lightly) that you are having a Hard Five Minutes — and your child is on care duty. Their job: run all four words. They will notice (you look tired), support (fetch the cushion), encourage (you can do the dishes — I mean, you have got this!) and comfort (the blanket, the pat, the sit-together). Accept every offering with full ceremony. Then swap: what helped MOST? The answer teaches you both.',
 tips:'Accept the child\u2019s comfort without trimming it — the offered half-flat cushion and the pat-pat-pat are exactly as healing as they feel to give; resist redirecting toward "proper" help. And keep the theatrical hard-day SMALL and safe: children comfort best when the storm is clearly survivable — the goal is their competence, not their worry.',
 check:'Ask: "Which word starts with noticing? What does support carry? What does comfort do instead of fixing?" Care, a corner, sits with you — three answers and the helping words are on duty.'},
'listen-understand-respond-forgive':{
 title:'The Listen, Understand, Respond & Forgive Lesson',one:'The four conversation words — ears all the way to the end, the say-back, the careful answer and the always-ready mend.',
 seoTitle:'Listening Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Listen, Understand, Respond, Forgive (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: teach the four conversation words — listen, understand, respond and forgive — with a two-minute listening date that upgrades every family conversation.',
 intro:'This lesson upgrades the family\u2019s conversations with four words in the right order: ears to the end, the say-back to prove it, the careful answer and the mend that is always ready.',
 what:'Listen runs to the end of the sentence, understand says back what it heard, respond answers with care and forgive keeps the mend on the shelf, ready for whenever the conversation wobbles.',
 mean:'Most preschool conflicts — and, honestly, most family conflicts — are two people talking and nobody listening. The say-back is the fix: you cannot say back what you did not hear, so the mechanic enforces the virtue.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: listening goes ALL THE WAY to the end (no finishing other people\u2019s sentences); understand is proven, not claimed (the say-back: so you felt left out?); and forgive stays warm on the shelf — the conversation that wobbles can always be mended mid-stream.',
 why:'This card assembles the class\u2019s final toolset: the earlier peace steps (listen first, think first) and friendship words (forgive) now run as one conversation system — the last skill before the graduation card.',
 dev:'Active listening (the full-ears skill), reflective responding (the say-back), conversational turn-taking, and repair-ready framing — keeping forgiveness within reach during, not only after, the wobble.',
 vocab:'listen, understand, respond, forgive, say back, all the way, mend',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The four conversation words."\n2) Read the panels: the ear-hand girl LISTENs, the nodding boy UNDERSTANDs, the gesturing boy RESPONDs, the peaceful girl FORGIVEs.\n3) Run the listening date below — two minutes, full ears, one say-back each.\n4) Drill the say-back formula on toy drama: bear A complains, bear B must say back ONE thing it heard before answering — "so you felt squashed."\n5) Close with the takeaway: "ears to the end, say it back, answer with care — and keep the forgiving word warm."',
 say:'"Conversations work best in an order. Listen — all the way to the end of what they are saying; not most of the way, not while rehearsing your answer: ALL the way. Understand — prove it with a say-back: so you felt left out when they ran off? A good say-back is worth ten guesses. Respond — now, and only now, your answer: careful, kind, yours. And forgive — keep the word warm the whole time, because conversations wobble sometimes; a misunderstanding, a stepped-on toe, a too-loud voice — the forgiving word is the mend that lets the conversation keep going. Ears, say-back, answer, mend. That is how people who love each other talk."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the conversation: Listen, a girl listening with a hand to her ear; Understand, a boy nodding with a finger to his chin; Respond, a boy answering with an open gesture; and Forgive, a girl with clasped hands and a peaceful smile — the system drawn as four friendly faces.',
 ask:'"How far does listening go?" "How do we PROVE we understood?" "What stays warm on the shelf?" "What is a say-back?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "So you felt sad when…" (the say-back, deployed on YOU) "I listened all the way!" or the mender: "I forgive you — now where were we?"',
 real:'Conversation moments run all day: the sibling\u2019s story that gets interrupted, the instruction half-heard, the hurt feeling half-answered. Install the say-back as the family norm — "before you answer, tell me one thing you heard" — and watch the interruptions starve.',
 play:'The Two-Minute Listening Date: once a day, two minutes, one speaker, one listener. The speaker talks about anything (the dream, the dinosaur, the outrage at lunch). The listener may not interrupt — then must say back ONE thing they heard before commenting. Swap. Grown-ups play too, and get their listening graded (kindly) by the four-year-old. Three dates a week and the say-back starts appearing in wild conversations, unsupervised.',
 tips:'Grade the say-back, not the sympathy: "good ears — you caught the dinosaur part" teaches the mechanic; "poor you" teaches nothing repeatable. And model the forgive-mid-stream: "wait — I think I answered too fast and hurt your feelings. I am sorry — say it again, my ears are working now." A child who sees mending happen DURING conversations learns that repair is normal maintenance, not an emergency procedure.',
 check:'Ask: "How far does listening go? What proves understanding? Where does forgive stay?" To the end, the say-back, warm on the shelf — three answers and the conversation system runs.'},
'kind-words-kind-actions-kind-heart-choose-kindness':{
 title:'The Kind Words, Kind Actions, Kind Heart & Choose Kindness Lesson',one:'The graduation card — kind on the tongue, kind in the hands, kind underneath, and the choice, freely made, every time.',
 seoTitle:'Kindness Graduation Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Kind Words, Actions, Heart, Choose Kindness (Class 38)',
 metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: the kindness graduation — kind words, kind actions, kind heart and choose kindness — the last lesson of Class 38, where kindness becomes a choice your child owns.',
 intro:'The last lesson of the class is also its first: kindness, in four layers — spoken, done, felt and CHOSEN. Everything the previous thirty-four cards taught gathers on this one.',
 what:'Kind words are the tongue\u2019s kindness, kind actions the hands\u2019, kind heart the underneath-feeling — and choose kindness is the free pick: nobody makes you kind; every moment, you get to choose it again.',
 mean:'The deepest finding of all kindness research fits on this card: kindness behaves like a muscle — every free pick strengthens it, until the picking stops looking like choices and starts looking like character. A child who knows the choice is THEIRS stops performing kindness for applause and starts owning it as identity.',
 know:'Your child needs three ideas: kindness runs in THREE layers (words, actions, heart — a kind word with a cold heart still counts for practice, the layers grow together); the choice is genuinely FREE (which is what makes it strong); and the choosing never retires — every single day hands you fresh picks.',
 why:'This is the graduation: thirty-four cards of feelings named, skills drilled, habits rehearsed — and here is what it was all for. The class does not end with a test; it ends with a picker, fully loaded.',
 dev:'Moral agency (kindness as free choice), values integration (words-actions-heart alignment), identity consolidation (I am someone who chooses kind), and the lifetime habit the whole class built: noticing, choosing, repeating.',
 vocab:'kind words, kind actions, kind heart, choose kindness, choice, graduation, every time',
 teach:'1) Show the card: "The graduation card — everything you have learned, in four layers."\n2) Read the panels: the speaking girl holds KIND WORDS, the hand-shaking boy KIND ACTIONS, the heart-holding boy KIND HEART, and the hugging pair CHOOSE KINDNESS — the only panel with two children in it, because chosen kindness is shared.\n3) Run the kindness hunt below: spot all three layers in one ordinary day.\n4) Ask the big question at bedtime: "what kindness will YOU choose tomorrow?" — and ask again in the morning. The asking IS the graduation.\n5) Close with the takeaway: "thirty-five cards, one sentence: kindness is a choice, and it is yours."',
 say:'"Here is the last card, and it holds everything. Kind words — the things we SAY: well done, want to play, thank you, I am sorry. Kind actions — the things we DO: the shared snack, the helped-up friend, the fetched cup. Kind heart — the underneath: the warm feeling that notices people and wants them okay. And choose kindness — the part nobody can do for you: every single day hands you moments, and every moment you get to PICK. Nobody makes you kind. You choose it. And here is the secret: choose it enough times and it stops feeling like a choice — it starts feeling like you. That is the whole graduation. That is who you are becoming."',
 notice:'On this card four panels hold the graduation: Kind Words, a girl speaking with a hand on her chest; Kind Actions, a boy offering his hand; Kind Heart, a boy holding a pink heart; and Choose Kindness, two children hugging — the only card where two figures share one panel, because the choice is contagious.',
 ask:'"Name one kind word, one kind action, one kind heart moment from today. Who does the choosing?" "What happens if we choose kind every day?"',
 childSay:'Your child might say: "Mia said a kind word to me at snack!" "I held the door — kind action!" or the graduated one: "tomorrow I choose… the new kid. I will say hi first."',
 real:'The three layers live one day apart: the compliment at breakfast (words), the shared umbrella on the walk (actions), the quiet noticing of the sad friend (heart), and the moment at the park when nobody is watching and the child shares anyway (the choice). Spot them, name the layer, and let the picking be praised above all.',
 play:'The Kindness Hunt: one ordinary day, three finds. Spot ONE kind word (anyone\u2019s — yours, a sibling\u2019s, a story character\u2019s), ONE kind action, ONE kind-heart moment. Report them at dinner like treasure. Then the big question: "what kindness will YOU choose tomorrow?" Write it down if you like. Ask again in the morning. Repeat for a week — and watch the picks get braver.',
 tips:'Praise the CHOICE louder than the outcome: "nobody made you share — you picked that" lands deeper than any sticker; agency is the engine. And when the child picks UNkind (they will; the choice must stay real), keep the door open: "that was a pick too. What pick would you like to make next?" The freedom is what makes the kindness theirs — guard it even when the pick is wrong.',
 check:'Ask: "Name the three layers of kindness. Who does the choosing? What does the choice become if we repeat it?" Words-actions-heart, me, you — three answers and the class is graduated. Thirty-five cards, one picker, fully loaded.'}
};